{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","prNumber":656,"prUrl":"https://github.com/faucet-hq/faucet-stream/pull/656","prRepository":"faucet-hq/faucet-stream","timestamp":"2026-09-17T14:12:39.670Z"}
{"parentUuid":"bc0f5ea0-b331-4d6c-8824-c03abe885888","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_non_blocking_error","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_018wDbJ6XoufnsQUs3ju2tAd","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","stderr":"Failed with non-blocking status code: /bin/sh: rtk: command not found","stdout":"","exitCode":127,"command":"rtk hook claude","durationMs":20823},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a5ba62ce-b646-4558-9dec-44658e41c0d3","timestamp":"2026-09-17T14:13:00.376Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"bc0f5ea0-b331-4d6c-8824-c03abe885888","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1e50a2b8-f79b-4f2b-a79b-e532971b731e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018wDbJ6XoufnsQUs3ju2tAd","type":"tool_result","content":"The findings from the principles audit (#654) that I was **certain** of: two silent data-loss paths, one silent duplicate-delivery path, one that starves the DLQ, and a core guard that didn't express the property it checked. The config renames and the semver rollout stay in #654 — they change users' YAML and deserve a deliberate decision, not a unilateral one.\n\n## The core defect (#654 C1)\n\n`with_retry_write!` guarded the **non-idempotent** write paths with `supports_idempotent_writes()`. That method promises only that rows *and a commit token* commit together on the `write_batch_idempotent` path — it says nothing about a plain `write_batch`. Eleven sinks advertise it while their ordinary write is a bare multi-row `INSERT`, so the macro retried exactly the writes its own comment says must never be retried:\n\n> *\"if the request commits server-side but the response is lost, a pipeline-level retry silently duplicates every row — the repo's #1 worst bug class (F29/F32)\"*\n\nAdds a defaulted `Sink::write_batch_is_replay_safe()` — conservative default: a keyed upsert/delete converges on replay, anything else does not — and gates on that. Additive and defaulted, so a **minor**; `cargo-semver-checks` confirms \"no semver update required\".\n\n## The three connector paths\n\n| | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Delta** (#654 C2) | `\"not found\"` / `\"no such file\"` / `\"does not exist\"` in rendered text meant \"no table here\". Object stores render those for **403 AccessDenied**, expired credentials, transient `_delta_log/` listing failures — so an *existing* table read as absent, the sink wrote a fresh `_delta_log/…0.json`, and every data file was orphaned. | Typed `DeltaTableError::ObjectStore { source: NotFound }`. |\n| **Spanner** (#654 C3) | A message-grep arm sat *below* the authoritative typed `Code::NotFound` check, so it only fired for `PermissionDenied`/`Unavailable`/`DeadlineExceeded` whose text mentioned the watermark table — reporting \"no token committed\" and **re-writing already-committed pages under `exactly_once`**. | Arm deleted; the typed check stands alone. |\n| **MSSQL** (#654 C4) | Transience from 7 lowercase substrings. SQL Server embeds user identifiers in error text, so `Violation of UNIQUE KEY constraint 'UQ_connection_id'` matched `\"connection\"` → called transient → propagated forever, and **the poison row never reached the DLQ**. `contains(\"1205\")` matched any message containing those digits. | Typed `ChunkFailure` from tiberius' error number/variant, classified where the type still exists. The local re-run is now restricted to codes the server **guarantees it rolled back** (1205 deadlock victim, 1222 lock-request timeout); everything outcome-unknown propagates instead of being re-run. Backoff comes from core's jittered helper. |\n\n## Also, small and additive\n\n- **`http`** (#654 H5): both retry arms `continue`d with **no sleep** — a brief 503 became an amplifying burst within microseconds. Now honours a server `Retry-After` when present, else core's capped jittered backoff.\n- **`clickhouse`** (#654 H6): a **durable** state key derived from `DefaultHasher`, whose output is not stable across Rust releases — a toolchain bump silently re-keyed the bookmark and re-replicated the whole table with a green exit code. Now core's FNV-1a `shard_hash`. ⚠️ **This changes the derived default key once, deliberately** — set an explicit `state_key` to keep an existing bookmark.\n- **`FaucetError` is `#[non_exhaustive]`** (#654 H11) — it gained four variants post-1.0 and is the type every connector author touches; the marker keeps the next one a minor. Whole workspace still compiles.\n- **`retry::apply_jitter` is now `pub`** (#654 M13) — it was `pub(crate)`, which is *why* four connectors hand-rolled unjittered copies. They can now consume it.\n- **`deny_unknown_fields`** on the `observability:` family and the `quality:` specs (#654 M18) — the only two blocks that missed it, and both fail **open**: a typo'd `prometheus.listten` silently yields no metrics; a mistyped quality check is a silently unenforced data gate.\n\n## Tests\n\nTwo existing retry tests used `supports_idempotent_writes` purely as the *enabling knob* (their comments said so). **Their assertions are unchanged** — only the mock's declared property moves, because the precondition itself was the bug. The old MSSQL string-grep test was deleted: its expectations encoded the defect.\n\nAdded: both halves of the new gate (a token-capable sink with a plain INSERT is *not* retried; a keyed-upsert sink *is*), the typed MSSQL classifier including the constraint-name-that-looks-transient case, `quote_columns`' failure classification, ClickHouse key stability, and both HTTP retry-wait arms.\n\n## Coverage — read this before the gate result\n\nLocal patch coverage is **82%**, and I want to be explicit about why rather than have it look like an oversight. The 24 remaining uncovered changed lines are all inside MSSQL's I/O shim (`insert_chunk`'s exec/timeout/rollback and the `write_batch` re-run loop). They need a live SQL Server, and **that container image is x86-only, so it cannot run on an ARM dev machine** — CI (x86 Linux) does run those tests. To avoid leaning on that, I pulled the genuinely pure parts *out* of the shim so they are unit-tested here: `classify_chunk_failure`, `quote_columns`, `retry_wait`. If CI's number is still short, the next step is a tiberius-level fake rather than a documented exception.\n\nGates run locally: `cargo fmt`, full-workspace `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings`, `cargo-semver-checks` on core, the committed schema test, and every touched crate's suite (Spanner's exactly-once conformance included, via colima).\n\nRefs #654.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"7ac6e670-298a-4189-a788-b837bd74d3ac","timestamp":"2026-09-17T14:13:03.535Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"The findings from the principles audit (#654) that I was **certain** of: two silent data-loss paths, one silent duplicate-delivery path, one that starves the DLQ, and a core guard that didn't express the property it checked. The config renames and the semver rollout stay in #654 — they change users' YAML and deserve a deliberate decision, not a unilateral one.\n\n## The core defect (#654 C1)\n\n`with_retry_write!` guarded the **non-idempotent** write paths with `supports_idempotent_writes()`. That method promises only that rows *and a commit token* commit together on the `write_batch_idempotent` path — it says nothing about a plain `write_batch`. Eleven sinks advertise it while their ordinary write is a bare multi-row `INSERT`, so the macro retried exactly the writes its own comment says must never be retried:\n\n> *\"if the request commits server-side but the response is lost, a pipeline-level retry silently duplicates every row — the repo's #1 worst bug class (F29/F32)\"*\n\nAdds a defaulted `Sink::write_batch_is_replay_safe()` — conservative default: a keyed upsert/delete converges on replay, anything else does not — and gates on that. Additive and defaulted, so a **minor**; `cargo-semver-checks` confirms \"no semver update required\".\n\n## The three connector paths\n\n| | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Delta** (#654 C2) | `\"not found\"` / `\"no such file\"` / `\"does not exist\"` in rendered text meant \"no table here\". Object stores render those for **403 AccessDenied**, expired credentials, transient `_delta_log/` listing failures — so an *existing* table read as absent, the sink wrote a fresh `_delta_log/…0.json`, and every data file was orphaned. | Typed `DeltaTableError::ObjectStore { source: NotFound }`. |\n| **Spanner** (#654 C3) | A message-grep arm sat *below* the authoritative typed `Code::NotFound` check, so it only fired for `PermissionDenied`/`Unavailable`/`DeadlineExceeded` whose text mentioned the watermark table — reporting \"no token committed\" and **re-writing already-committed pages under `exactly_once`**. | Arm deleted; the typed check stands alone. |\n| **MSSQL** (#654 C4) | Transience from 7 lowercase substrings. SQL Server embeds user identifiers in error text, so `Violation of UNIQUE KEY constraint 'UQ_connection_id'` matched `\"connection\"` → called transient → propagated forever, and **the poison row never reached the DLQ**. `contains(\"1205\")` matched any message containing those digits. | Typed `ChunkFailure` from tiberius' error number/variant, classified where the type still exists. The local re-run is now restricted to codes the server **guarantees it rolled back** (1205 deadlock victim, 1222 lock-request timeout); everything outcome-unknown propagates instead of being re-run. Backoff comes from core's jittered helper. |\n\n## Also, small and additive\n\n- **`http`** (#654 H5): both retry arms `continue`d with **no sleep** — a brief 503 became an amplifying burst within microseconds. Now honours a server `Retry-After` when present, else core's capped jittered backoff.\n- **`clickhouse`** (#654 H6): a **durable** state key derived from `DefaultHasher`, whose output is not stable across Rust releases — a toolchain bump silently re-keyed the bookmark and re-replicated the whole table with a green exit code. Now core's FNV-1a `shard_hash`. ⚠️ **This changes the derived default key once, deliberately** — set an explicit `state_key` to keep an existing bookmark.\n- **`FaucetError` is `#[non_exhaustive]`** (#654 H11) — it gained four variants post-1.0 and is the type every connector author touches; the marker keeps the next one a minor. Whole workspace still compiles.\n- **`retry::apply_jitter` is now `pub`** (#654 M13) — it was `pub(crate)`, which is *why* four connectors hand-rolled unjittered copies. They can now consume it.\n- **`deny_unknown_fields`** on the `observability:` family and the `quality:` specs (#654 M18) — the only two blocks that missed it, and both fail **open**: a typo'd `prometheus.listten` silently yields no metrics; a mistyped quality check is a silently unenforced data gate.\n\n## Tests\n\nTwo existing retry tests used `supports_idempotent_writes` purely as the *enabling knob* (their comments said so). **Their assertions are unchanged** — only the mock's declared property moves, because the precondition itself was the bug. The old MSSQL string-grep test was deleted: its expectations encoded the defect.\n\nAdded: both halves of the new gate (a token-capable sink with a plain INSERT is *not* retried; a keyed-upsert sink *is*), the typed MSSQL classifier including the constraint-name-that-looks-transient case, `quote_columns`' failure classification, ClickHouse key stability, and both HTTP retry-wait arms.\n\n## Coverage — read this before the gate result\n\nLocal patch coverage is **82%**, and I want to be explicit about why rather than have it look like an oversight. The 24 remaining uncovered changed lines are all inside MSSQL's I/O shim (`insert_chunk`'s exec/timeout/rollback and the `write_batch` re-run loop). They need a live SQL Server, and **that container image is x86-only, so it cannot run on an ARM dev machine** — CI (x86 Linux) does run those tests. To avoid leaning on that, I pulled the genuinely pure parts *out* of the shim so they are unit-tested here: `classify_chunk_failure`, `quote_columns`, `retry_wait`. If CI's number is still short, the next step is a tiberius-level fake rather than a documented exception.\n\nGates run locally: `cargo fmt`, full-workspace `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings`, `cargo-semver-checks` on core, the committed schema test, and every touched crate's suite (Spanner's exactly-once conformance included, via colima).\n\nRefs #654.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bc0f5ea0-b331-4d6c-8824-c03abe885888","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
