{"parentUuid":"1afbd57b-cd16-401c-bed8-ed165e8a328a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_015ZLZqF3TQZno2c8MhJxqb2","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"faucet-stream\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Bash\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/c-sikawats/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.2.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":255},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4fdcd0df-534f-4545-a56d-d6cfa3e7ba2d","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:28:19.529Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/cover-pure-decisions","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"4fdcd0df-534f-4545-a56d-d6cfa3e7ba2d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/tests/reliability_bookmark_ordering.rs","snippet":"1\t//! #651 Category A1 — bookmark ordering and crash safety.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The guarantee: **a bookmark is persisted only after the sink has confirmed\n4\t//! the data that bookmark covers.** Everything downstream of faucet depends on\n5\t//! it — it is what makes a resumed run lose nothing.\n6\t//!\n7\t//! A unit test on pure logic cannot check this, because the guarantee is not a\n8\t//! property of any one function: it is a property of the *order* in which the\n9\t//! engine calls two collaborators. So these tests run the real `Pipeline::run`\n10\t//! against the scripted doubles and assert against the interleaved event log.\n11\t//!\n12\t//! Each test states the failure it injects and what must be true afterwards.\n13\t//! Where a test would still pass if the guarantee were broken, it says so and\n14\t//! asserts the stronger thing instead.\n15\t\n16\tuse std::sync::Arc;\n17\t\n18\tuse faucet_conformance::scripted::{\n19\t    Boundary, Event, EventLog, PagedSource, ScriptedSink, assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes,\n20\t};\n21\tuse faucet_core::{Pipeline, Source, Value};\n22\t\n23\t/// The bookmark the engine persists for `PagedSource` after page `p`.\n24\tfn bookmark_for_page(p: usize) -> Value {\n25\t    serde_json::json!({ \"page\": p })\n26\t}\n27\t\n28\t/// Records covered by pages `0..=p`.\n29\tfn records_through_page(p: usize, per_page: usize) -> usize {\n30\t    (p + 1) * per_page\n31\t}\n32\t\n33\t#[tokio::test]\n34\tasync fn every_bookmark_is_backed_by_confirmed_writes_on_a_clean_run() {\n35\t    let log = EventLog::new();\n36\t    let sink = ScriptedSink::new(log.clone());\n37\t    let store = Arc::new(sink.state_store());\n38\t    let source = PagedSource::new(4, 5);\n39\t\n40\t    let result = Pipeline::new(&source, &sink)\n41\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n42\t        .run()\n43\t        .await\n44\t        .expect(\"a clean run succeeds\");\n45\t\n46\t    assert_eq!(result.records_written, 20);\n47\t\n48\t    // The shared guarantee assertion — the same one a deliberately-wrong event\n49\t    // log is fed in `scripted`'s own `#[should_panic]` tests, so this is a\n50\t    // check that has been shown capable of failing.\n51\t    assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(&log, |b| {\n52\t        records_through_page(b[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize, 5)\n53\t    });\n54\t\n55\t    // The guarantee, stated directly: at the moment each bookmark was made\n56\t    // durable, the sink had already confirmed exactly the records that\n57\t    // bookmark claims.\n58\t    let confirmed_at_each = log.writes_before_each_bookmark();\n59\t    let bookmarks = log.bookmarks();\n60\t    assert_eq!(\n61\t        bookmarks.len(),\n62\t        4,\n63\t        \"one bookmark per page: {:?}\",\n64\t        log.events()\n65\t    );\n66\t    for (i, (confirmed, bookmark)) in confirmed_at_each.iter().zip(&bookmarks).enumerate() {\n67\t        let page = bookmark[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize;\n68\t        assert_eq!(page, i, \"bookmarks must advance in page order\");\n69\t        assert!(\n70\t            *confirmed >= records_through_page(page, 5),\n71\t            \"bookmark {bookmark} was persisted with only {confirmed} records confirmed — it \\\n72\t             claims {} are durable\",\n73\t            records_through_page(page, 5)\n74\t        );\n75\t    }\n76\t}\n77\t\n78\t#[tokio::test]\n79\tasync fn a_failed_write_never_advances_the_bookmark_past_it() {\n80\t    // Page 2's write fails. Pages 0 and 1 were confirmed, so their bookmarks\n81\t    // are legitimate; page 2's must never become durable.\n82\t    let log = EventLog::new();\n83\t    let sink = ScriptedSink::new(log.clone()).failing_at(Boundary::Write(2));\n84\t    let store = Arc::new(sink.state_store());\n85\t    let source = PagedSource::new(5, 4);\n86\t\n87\t    let err = Pipeline::new(&source, &sink)\n88\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n89\t        .run()\n90\t        .await\n91\t        .expect_err(\"the run must surface the sink failure, not swallow it\");\n92\t    assert!(\n93\t        matches!(err, faucet_core::FaucetError::Sink(_)),\n94\t        \"expected the sink's own typed error, got {err:?}\"\n95\t    );\n96\t\n97\t    let durable = store\n98\t        .stored(\"scripted:paged\")\n99\t        .expect(\"the confirmed pages left a bookmark behind\");\n100\t    let page = durable[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize;\n101\t    assert!(\n102\t        page < 2,\n103\t        \"the durable bookmark is {durable}, which covers the page whose write FAILED — a \\\n104\t         resumed run would skip it and lose those records\"\n105\t    );\n106\t\n107\t    // And the log must show no bookmark at all after the failure.\n108\t    assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(&log, |b| {\n109\t        records_through_page(b[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize, 4)\n110\t    });\n111\t\n112\t    let fail_at = log\n113\t        .position(|e| matches!(e, Event::WriteFailed(_)))\n114\t        .expect(\"the failure is logged\");\n115\t    let after = &log.events()[fail_at..];\n116\t    assert!(\n117\t        !after.iter().any(|e| matches!(e, Event::StatePut { .. })),\n118\t        \"a bookmark was persisted after the write failed: {after:?}\"\n119\t    );\n120\t}\n121\t\n122\t#[tokio::test]\n123\tasync fn a_failed_flush_does_not_leave_a_bookmark_for_unflushed_data() {\n124\t    // The subtle one: the writes all *succeeded*, so a naive engine would\n125\t    // happily persist the bookmark. But flush is what makes them durable, and\n126\t    // it failed — so the data is not there and the bookmark must not claim it.\n127\t    let log = EventLog::new();\n128\t    let sink = ScriptedSink::new(log.clone()).failing_at(Boundary::Flush);\n129\t    let store = Arc::new(sink.state_store());\n130\t    let source = PagedSource::new(3, 4);\n131\t\n132\t    let err = Pipeline::new(&source, &sink)\n133\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n134\t        .run()\n135\t        .await\n136\t        .expect_err(\"a failed flush must fail the run\");\n137\t    assert!(\n138\t        matches!(err, faucet_core::FaucetError::Sink(_)),\n139\t        \"got {err:?}\"\n140\t    );\n141\t\n142\t    assert!(\n143\t        log.any(|e| matches!(e, Event::FlushFailed)),\n144\t        \"the flush failure must actually have been exercised: {:?}\",\n145\t        log.events()\n146\t    );\n147\t    assert_eq!(\n148\t        store.stored(\"scripted:paged\"),\n149\t        None,\n150\t        \"no page was ever flushed, so nothing may be claimed durable\"\n151\t    );\n152\t}\n153\t\n154\t#[tokio::test]\n155\tasync fn resume_re_reads_exactly_the_unconfirmed_tail() {\n156\t    let per_page = 4;\n157\t\n158\t    // Run 1: page 3 of 6 fails.\n159\t    let log1 = EventLog::new();\n160\t    let sink1 = ScriptedSink::new(log1.clone()).failing_at(Boundary::Write(3));\n161\t    let store = Arc::new(sink1.state_store());\n162\t    let source1 = PagedSource::new(6, per_page);\n163\t    Pipeline::new(&source1, &sink1)\n164\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n165\t        .run()\n166\t        .await\n167\t        .expect_err(\"run 1 fails mid-stream\");\n168\t\n169\t    let durable = store.stored(\"scripted:paged\").expect(\"partial progress\");\n170\t    let resumed_from = durable[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page\") as usize;\n171\t    let confirmed_run1 = log1.records_written();\n172\t\n173\t    // Run 2: same durable store, a healthy sink.\n174\t    let log2 = EventLog::new();\n175\t    let sink2 = ScriptedSink::new(log2.clone());\n176\t    let source2 = PagedSource::new(6, per_page);\n177\t    let result = Pipeline::new(&source2, &sink2)\n178\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n179\t        .run()\n180\t        .await\n181\t        .expect(\"run 2 completes\");\n182\t\n183\t    // It restarted from the page *after* the last durable bookmark — not from\n184\t    // the beginning (which would be wasteful but safe) and not past the gap\n185\t    // (which would lose data).\n186\t    assert_eq!(\n187\t        source2.emitted_pages().first().copied(),\n188\t        Some(resumed_from + 1),\n189\t        \"resume must start at the page after the durable bookmark; emitted {:?}\",\n190\t        source2.emitted_pages()\n191\t    );\n192\t\n193\t    // Nothing is lost: every page is covered by run 1 or run 2.\n194\t    let mut covered: Vec<usize> = source1.emitted_pages();\n195\t    covered.extend(source2.emitted_pages());\n196\t    covered.sort_unstable();\n197\t    covered.dedup();\n198\t    assert_eq!(\n199\t        covered,\n200\t        (0..6).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n\n... [140 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"da84813f-6ad0-477b-bb01-0c8f38e776b7","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:28:19.870Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/tests/reliability_bookmark_ordering.rs changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t//! #651 Category A1 — bookmark ordering and crash safety.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The guarantee: **a bookmark is persisted only after the sink has confirmed\n4\t//! the data that bookmark covers.** Everything downstream of faucet depends on\n5\t//! it — it is what makes a resumed run lose nothing.\n6\t//!\n7\t//! A unit test on pure logic cannot check this, because the guarantee is not a\n8\t//! property of any one function: it is a property of the *order* in which the\n9\t//! engine calls two collaborators. So these tests run the real `Pipeline::run`\n10\t//! against the scripted doubles and assert against the interleaved event log.\n11\t//!\n12\t//! Each test states the failure it injects and what must be true afterwards.\n13\t//! Where a test would still pass if the guarantee were broken, it says so and\n14\t//! asserts the stronger thing instead.\n15\t\n16\tuse std::sync::Arc;\n17\t\n18\tuse faucet_conformance::scripted::{\n19\t    Boundary, Event, EventLog, PagedSource, ScriptedSink, assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes,\n20\t};\n21\tuse faucet_core::{Pipeline, Source, Value};\n22\t\n23\t/// The bookmark the engine persists for `PagedSource` after page `p`.\n24\tfn bookmark_for_page(p: usize) -> Value {\n25\t    serde_json::json!({ \"page\": p })\n26\t}\n27\t\n28\t/// Records covered by pages `0..=p`.\n29\tfn records_through_page(p: usize, per_page: usize) -> usize {\n30\t    (p + 1) * per_page\n31\t}\n32\t\n33\t#[tokio::test]\n34\tasync fn every_bookmark_is_backed_by_confirmed_writes_on_a_clean_run() {\n35\t    let log = EventLog::new();\n36\t    let sink = ScriptedSink::new(log.clone());\n37\t    let store = Arc::new(sink.state_store());\n38\t    let source = PagedSource::new(4, 5);\n39\t\n40\t    let result = Pipeline::new(&source, &sink)\n41\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n42\t        .run()\n43\t        .await\n44\t        .expect(\"a clean run succeeds\");\n45\t\n46\t    assert_eq!(result.records_written, 20);\n47\t\n48\t    // The shared guarantee assertion — the same one a deliberately-wrong event\n49\t    // log is fed in `scripted`'s own `#[should_panic]` tests, so this is a\n50\t    // check that has been shown capable of failing.\n51\t    assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(&log, |b| {\n52\t        records_through_page(b[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize, 5)\n53\t    });\n54\t\n55\t    // The guarantee, stated directly: at the moment each bookmark was made\n56\t    // durable, the sink had already confirmed exactly the records that\n57\t    // bookmark claims.\n58\t    let confirmed_at_each = log.writes_before_each_bookmark();\n59\t    let bookmarks = log.bookmarks();\n60\t    assert_eq!(\n61\t        bookmarks.len(),\n62\t        4,\n63\t        \"one bookmark per page: {:?}\",\n64\t        log.events()\n65\t    );\n66\t    for (i, (confirmed, bookmark)) in confirmed_at_each.iter().zip(&bookmarks).enumerate() {\n67\t        let page = bookmark[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize;\n68\t        assert_eq!(page, i, \"bookmarks must advance in page order\");\n69\t        assert!(\n70\t            *confirmed >= records_through_page(page, 5),\n71\t            \"bookmark {bookmark} was persisted with only {confirmed} records confirmed — it \\\n72\t             claims {} are durable\",\n73\t            records_through_page(page, 5)\n74\t        );\n75\t    }\n76\t}\n77\t\n78\t#[tokio::test]\n79\tasync fn a_failed_write_never_advances_the_bookmark_past_it() {\n80\t    // Page 2's write fails. Pages 0 and 1 were confirmed, so their bookmarks\n81\t    // are legitimate; page 2's must never become durable.\n82\t    let log = EventLog::new();\n83\t    let sink = ScriptedSink::new(log.clone()).failing_at(Boundary::Write(2));\n84\t    let store = Arc::new(sink.state_store());\n85\t    let source = PagedSource::new(5, 4);\n86\t\n87\t    let err = Pipeline::new(&source, &sink)\n88\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n89\t        .run()\n90\t        .await\n91\t        .expect_err(\"the run must surface the sink failure, not swallow it\");\n92\t    assert!(\n93\t        matches!(err, faucet_core::FaucetError::Sink(_)),\n94\t        \"expected the sink's own typed error, got {err:?}\"\n95\t    );\n96\t\n97\t    let durable = store\n98\t        .stored(\"scripted:paged\")\n99\t        .expect(\"the confirmed pages left a bookmark behind\");\n100\t    let page = durable[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize;\n101\t    assert!(\n102\t        page < 2,\n103\t        \"the durable bookmark is {durable}, which covers the page whose write FAILED — a \\\n104\t         resumed run would skip it and lose those records\"\n105\t    );\n106\t\n107\t    // And the log must show no bookmark at all after the failure.\n108\t    assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(&log, |b| {\n109\t        records_through_page(b[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page bookmark\") as usize, 4)\n110\t    });\n111\t\n112\t    let fail_at = log\n113\t        .position(|e| matches!(e, Event::WriteFailed(_)))\n114\t        .expect(\"the failure is logged\");\n115\t    let after = &log.events()[fail_at..];\n116\t    assert!(\n117\t        !after.iter().any(|e| matches!(e, Event::StatePut { .. })),\n118\t        \"a bookmark was persisted after the write failed: {after:?}\"\n119\t    );\n120\t}\n121\t\n122\t#[tokio::test]\n123\tasync fn a_failed_flush_does_not_leave_a_bookmark_for_unflushed_data() {\n124\t    // The subtle one: the writes all *succeeded*, so a naive engine would\n125\t    // happily persist the bookmark. But flush is what makes them durable, and\n126\t    // it failed — so the data is not there and the bookmark must not claim it.\n127\t    let log = EventLog::new();\n128\t    let sink = ScriptedSink::new(log.clone()).failing_at(Boundary::Flush);\n129\t    let store = Arc::new(sink.state_store());\n130\t    let source = PagedSource::new(3, 4);\n131\t\n132\t    let err = Pipeline::new(&source, &sink)\n133\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n134\t        .run()\n135\t        .await\n136\t        .expect_err(\"a failed flush must fail the run\");\n137\t    assert!(\n138\t        matches!(err, faucet_core::FaucetError::Sink(_)),\n139\t        \"got {err:?}\"\n140\t    );\n141\t\n142\t    assert!(\n143\t        log.any(|e| matches!(e, Event::FlushFailed)),\n144\t        \"the flush failure must actually have been exercised: {:?}\",\n145\t        log.events()\n146\t    );\n147\t    assert_eq!(\n148\t        store.stored(\"scripted:paged\"),\n149\t        None,\n150\t        \"no page was ever flushed, so nothing may be claimed durable\"\n151\t    );\n152\t}\n153\t\n154\t#[tokio::test]\n155\tasync fn resume_re_reads_exactly_the_unconfirmed_tail() {\n156\t    let per_page = 4;\n157\t\n158\t    // Run 1: page 3 of 6 fails.\n159\t    let log1 = EventLog::new();\n160\t    let sink1 = ScriptedSink::new(log1.clone()).failing_at(Boundary::Write(3));\n161\t    let store = Arc::new(sink1.state_store());\n162\t    let source1 = PagedSource::new(6, per_page);\n163\t    Pipeline::new(&source1, &sink1)\n164\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n165\t        .run()\n166\t        .await\n167\t        .expect_err(\"run 1 fails mid-stream\");\n168\t\n169\t    let durable = store.stored(\"scripted:paged\").expect(\"partial progress\");\n170\t    let resumed_from = durable[\"page\"].as_u64().expect(\"page\") as usize;\n171\t    let confirmed_run1 = log1.records_written();\n172\t\n173\t    // Run 2: same durable store, a healthy sink.\n174\t    let log2 = EventLog::new();\n175\t    let sink2 = ScriptedSink::new(log2.clone());\n176\t    let source2 = PagedSource::new(6, per_page);\n177\t    let result = Pipeline::new(&source2, &sink2)\n178\t        .with_state_store(store.clone())\n179\t        .run()\n180\t        .await\n181\t        .expect(\"run 2 completes\");\n182\t\n183\t    // It restarted from the page *after* the last durable bookmark — not from\n184\t    // the beginning (which would be wasteful but safe) and not past the gap\n185\t    // (which would lose data).\n186\t    assert_eq!(\n187\t        source2.emitted_pages().first().copied(),\n188\t        Some(resumed_from + 1),\n189\t        \"resume must start at the page after the durable bookmark; emitted {:?}\",\n190\t        source2.emitted_pages()\n191\t    );\n192\t\n193\t    // Nothing is lost: every page is covered by run 1 or run 2.\n194\t    let mut covered: Vec<usize> = source1.emitted_pages();\n195\t    covered.extend(source2.emitted_pages());\n196\t    covered.sort_unstable();\n197\t    covered.dedup();\n198\t    assert_eq!(\n199\t        covered,\n200\t        (0..6).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n\n... [140 lines truncated] ...\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/cover-pure-decisions","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"da84813f-6ad0-477b-bb01-0c8f38e776b7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/src/scripted.rs","snippet":"1\t//! Boundary-precise failure injection for the reliability suites (#651\n2\t//! Category A).\n3\t//!\n4\t//! The engine's guarantees are all statements about **ordering across a\n5\t//! boundary** — \"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms\", \"the\n6\t//! staging table is swapped only after a fully successful run\", \"a cancel still\n7\t//! flushes\". A unit test over pure logic cannot observe an ordering, and a\n8\t//! hand-rolled `FailingSink` that fails on *every* call cannot fail at one\n9\t//! specific boundary. So each such test used to re-invent a slightly different\n10\t//! double, and the interesting case — fail on the *third* write, succeed\n11\t//! before and after — was rarely covered.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! This module supplies the two things those tests actually need:\n14\t//!\n15\t//! - [`EventLog`] — one shared, ordered record of everything the sink and the\n16\t//!   state store did. Assertions are then written against the *sequence*\n17\t//!   (`writes_before_each_bookmark`), which is what the guarantee says, rather\n18\t//!   than against a final count, which is what a weaker test checks.\n19\t//! - [`ScriptedSink`] / [`ScriptedStateStore`] — doubles that fail (or stall)\n20\t//!   at exactly one named [`Boundary`] and behave normally everywhere else.\n21\t//!\n22\t//! Both are cheap clones over shared state, so a test keeps a handle to inspect\n23\t//! after moving the double into the pipeline.\n24\t\n25\tuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n26\tuse std::time::Duration;\n27\t\n28\tuse faucet_core::{FaucetError, Sink, StateStore, Value, async_trait, json};\n29\t\n30\t/// One thing that happened, in the order it happened.\n31\t///\n32\t/// The variants deliberately carry the *payload size or identity* rather than\n33\t/// the payload: an assertion about ordering needs to know that write #3 landed\n34\t/// before the bookmark that followed it, not what was in it.\n35\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n36\tpub enum Event {\n37\t    /// `write_batch` (or one of its idempotent / partial variants) succeeded\n38\t    /// with this many records.\n39\t    Write(usize),\n40\t    /// A write was attempted and the script failed it. Carries the record count\n41\t    /// so a test can tell *which* batch was rejected.\n42\t    WriteFailed(usize),\n43\t    /// `flush` succeeded.\n44\t    Flush,\n45\t    /// `flush` was attempted and the script failed it.\n46\t    FlushFailed,\n47\t    /// `write_batch_idempotent` succeeded, carrying the commit token the sink\n48\t    /// stored. The token is the one thing a watermark test must see verbatim.\n49\t    IdempotentWrite { records: usize, token: String },\n50\t    /// `begin_overwrite` succeeded.\n51\t    BeginOverwrite,\n52\t    /// `commit_overwrite` succeeded — the atomic swap actually happened.\n53\t    CommitOverwrite,\n54\t    /// `abort_overwrite` succeeded — the staged data was discarded.\n55\t    AbortOverwrite,\n56\t    /// `StateStore::put` succeeded for `key`, carrying the persisted value so a\n57\t    /// test can assert *which* bookmark was durable at that point.\n58\t    StatePut { key: String, value: Value },\n59\t    /// A `StateStore::put` was attempted and the script failed it.\n60\t    StatePutFailed { key: String },\n61\t    /// `cleanup_scope` ran with this many tracked keys.\n62\t    Cleanup(usize),\n63\t}\n64\t\n65\t/// The shared, ordered event log. Clone it to keep a handle after the doubles\n66\t/// are moved into the pipeline.\n67\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]\n68\tpub struct EventLog(Arc<Mutex<Vec<Event>>>);\n69\t\n70\timpl EventLog {\n71\t    /// A fresh, empty log.\n72\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n73\t        Self::default()\n74\t    }\n75\t\n76\t    /// Append one event. Never blocks on a poisoned lock — a panicking test\n77\t    /// should fail on its own assertion, not on a secondary lock panic.\n78\t    pub fn push(&self, event: Event) {\n79\t        if let Ok(mut g) = self.0.lock() {\n80\t            g.push(event);\n81\t        }\n82\t    }\n83\t\n84\t    /// Every event, in order.\n85\t    pub fn events(&self) -> Vec<Event> {\n86\t        self.0.lock().map(|g| g.clone()).unwrap_or_default()\n87\t    }\n88\t\n89\t    /// Total records across successful writes of every kind.\n90\t    pub fn records_written(&self) -> usize {\n91\t        self.events()\n92\t            .iter()\n93\t            .map(|e| match e {\n94\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => *n,\n95\t                _ => 0,\n96\t            })\n97\t            .sum()\n98\t    }\n99\t\n100\t    /// The values persisted by `StateStore::put`, in order.\n101\t    pub fn bookmarks(&self) -> Vec<Value> {\n102\t        self.events()\n103\t            .iter()\n104\t            .filter_map(|e| match e {\n105\t                Event::StatePut { value, .. } => Some(value.clone()),\n106\t                _ => None,\n107\t            })\n108\t            .collect()\n109\t    }\n110\t\n111\t    /// For each successful `StateStore::put`, how many records had been\n112\t    /// **confirmed written** before it.\n113\t    ///\n114\t    /// This is the shape the core durability guarantee is stated in: a bookmark\n115\t    /// must never be persisted covering data the sink has not confirmed. A test\n116\t    /// asserts the returned counts against the data each bookmark claims.\n117\t    pub fn writes_before_each_bookmark(&self) -> Vec<usize> {\n118\t        let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n119\t        let mut out = Vec::new();\n120\t        for e in self.events() {\n121\t            match e {\n122\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n123\t                Event::StatePut { .. } => out.push(confirmed),\n124\t                _ => {}\n125\t            }\n126\t        }\n127\t        out\n128\t    }\n129\t\n130\t    /// Whether `event` occurs anywhere in the log.\n131\t    pub fn contains(&self, event: &Event) -> bool {\n132\t        self.events().iter().any(|e| e == event)\n133\t    }\n134\t\n135\t    /// Whether any event matches `pred` — for variants carrying a payload a\n136\t    /// test does not want to spell out in full.\n137\t    pub fn any(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> bool {\n138\t        self.events().iter().any(pred)\n139\t    }\n140\t\n141\t    /// Index of the first event matching `pred`, for order assertions.\n142\t    pub fn position(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> Option<usize> {\n143\t        self.events().iter().position(pred)\n144\t    }\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/// Assert the core durability guarantee over an [`EventLog`]: **no bookmark is\n148\t/// persisted before the sink confirmed the data that bookmark claims.**\n149\t///\n150\t/// `records_claimed` maps a persisted bookmark to the number of records it\n151\t/// asserts are durable. A bookmark that claims more than had been confirmed at\n152\t/// the moment it was written is a data-loss bug: a resumed run starts after it\n153\t/// and the unconfirmed records are never read again.\n154\t///\n155\t/// This lives here, rather than inline in each test, so the *same* assertion\n156\t/// can be pointed at a deliberately-wrong event log — which is how the suites\n157\t/// prove the check can actually fail (see this module's tests).\n158\tpub fn assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(\n159\t    log: &EventLog,\n160\t    records_claimed: impl Fn(&Value) -> usize,\n161\t) {\n162\t    let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n163\t    for (i, event) in log.events().into_iter().enumerate() {\n164\t        match event {\n165\t            Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n166\t            Event::StatePut { ref value, .. } => {\n167\t                let claimed = records_claimed(value);\n168\t                assert!(\n169\t                    claimed <= confirmed,\n170\t                    \"durability violated at event {i}: bookmark {value} claims {claimed} \\\n171\t                     records are durable, but only {confirmed} had been confirmed by the sink. \\\n172\t                     A resumed run would start after this bookmark and never re-read the \\\n173\t                     difference.\\nfull log: {:?}\",\n174\t                    log.events()\n175\t                );\n176\t            }\n177\t            _ => {}\n178\t        }\n179\t    }\n180\t}\n181\t\n182\t/// The boundary a script fails at.\n183\t///\n184\t/// Every variant names a real transition the engine makes, and each one is a\n185\t/// place a naive implementation gets the ordering wrong.\n\n... [731 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"242ece70-2797-4503-86ae-44f13ec193ed","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:28:19.871Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/src/scripted.rs changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t//! Boundary-precise failure injection for the reliability suites (#651\n2\t//! Category A).\n3\t//!\n4\t//! The engine's guarantees are all statements about **ordering across a\n5\t//! boundary** — \"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms\", \"the\n6\t//! staging table is swapped only after a fully successful run\", \"a cancel still\n7\t//! flushes\". A unit test over pure logic cannot observe an ordering, and a\n8\t//! hand-rolled `FailingSink` that fails on *every* call cannot fail at one\n9\t//! specific boundary. So each such test used to re-invent a slightly different\n10\t//! double, and the interesting case — fail on the *third* write, succeed\n11\t//! before and after — was rarely covered.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! This module supplies the two things those tests actually need:\n14\t//!\n15\t//! - [`EventLog`] — one shared, ordered record of everything the sink and the\n16\t//!   state store did. Assertions are then written against the *sequence*\n17\t//!   (`writes_before_each_bookmark`), which is what the guarantee says, rather\n18\t//!   than against a final count, which is what a weaker test checks.\n19\t//! - [`ScriptedSink`] / [`ScriptedStateStore`] — doubles that fail (or stall)\n20\t//!   at exactly one named [`Boundary`] and behave normally everywhere else.\n21\t//!\n22\t//! Both are cheap clones over shared state, so a test keeps a handle to inspect\n23\t//! after moving the double into the pipeline.\n24\t\n25\tuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n26\tuse std::time::Duration;\n27\t\n28\tuse faucet_core::{FaucetError, Sink, StateStore, Value, async_trait, json};\n29\t\n30\t/// One thing that happened, in the order it happened.\n31\t///\n32\t/// The variants deliberately carry the *payload size or identity* rather than\n33\t/// the payload: an assertion about ordering needs to know that write #3 landed\n34\t/// before the bookmark that followed it, not what was in it.\n35\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n36\tpub enum Event {\n37\t    /// `write_batch` (or one of its idempotent / partial variants) succeeded\n38\t    /// with this many records.\n39\t    Write(usize),\n40\t    /// A write was attempted and the script failed it. Carries the record count\n41\t    /// so a test can tell *which* batch was rejected.\n42\t    WriteFailed(usize),\n43\t    /// `flush` succeeded.\n44\t    Flush,\n45\t    /// `flush` was attempted and the script failed it.\n46\t    FlushFailed,\n47\t    /// `write_batch_idempotent` succeeded, carrying the commit token the sink\n48\t    /// stored. The token is the one thing a watermark test must see verbatim.\n49\t    IdempotentWrite { records: usize, token: String },\n50\t    /// `begin_overwrite` succeeded.\n51\t    BeginOverwrite,\n52\t    /// `commit_overwrite` succeeded — the atomic swap actually happened.\n53\t    CommitOverwrite,\n54\t    /// `abort_overwrite` succeeded — the staged data was discarded.\n55\t    AbortOverwrite,\n56\t    /// `StateStore::put` succeeded for `key`, carrying the persisted value so a\n57\t    /// test can assert *which* bookmark was durable at that point.\n58\t    StatePut { key: String, value: Value },\n59\t    /// A `StateStore::put` was attempted and the script failed it.\n60\t    StatePutFailed { key: String },\n61\t    /// `cleanup_scope` ran with this many tracked keys.\n62\t    Cleanup(usize),\n63\t}\n64\t\n65\t/// The shared, ordered event log. Clone it to keep a handle after the doubles\n66\t/// are moved into the pipeline.\n67\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]\n68\tpub struct EventLog(Arc<Mutex<Vec<Event>>>);\n69\t\n70\timpl EventLog {\n71\t    /// A fresh, empty log.\n72\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n73\t        Self::default()\n74\t    }\n75\t\n76\t    /// Append one event. Never blocks on a poisoned lock — a panicking test\n77\t    /// should fail on its own assertion, not on a secondary lock panic.\n78\t    pub fn push(&self, event: Event) {\n79\t        if let Ok(mut g) = self.0.lock() {\n80\t            g.push(event);\n81\t        }\n82\t    }\n83\t\n84\t    /// Every event, in order.\n85\t    pub fn events(&self) -> Vec<Event> {\n86\t        self.0.lock().map(|g| g.clone()).unwrap_or_default()\n87\t    }\n88\t\n89\t    /// Total records across successful writes of every kind.\n90\t    pub fn records_written(&self) -> usize {\n91\t        self.events()\n92\t            .iter()\n93\t            .map(|e| match e {\n94\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => *n,\n95\t                _ => 0,\n96\t            })\n97\t            .sum()\n98\t    }\n99\t\n100\t    /// The values persisted by `StateStore::put`, in order.\n101\t    pub fn bookmarks(&self) -> Vec<Value> {\n102\t        self.events()\n103\t            .iter()\n104\t            .filter_map(|e| match e {\n105\t                Event::StatePut { value, .. } => Some(value.clone()),\n106\t                _ => None,\n107\t            })\n108\t            .collect()\n109\t    }\n110\t\n111\t    /// For each successful `StateStore::put`, how many records had been\n112\t    /// **confirmed written** before it.\n113\t    ///\n114\t    /// This is the shape the core durability guarantee is stated in: a bookmark\n115\t    /// must never be persisted covering data the sink has not confirmed. A test\n116\t    /// asserts the returned counts against the data each bookmark claims.\n117\t    pub fn writes_before_each_bookmark(&self) -> Vec<usize> {\n118\t        let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n119\t        let mut out = Vec::new();\n120\t        for e in self.events() {\n121\t            match e {\n122\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n123\t                Event::StatePut { .. } => out.push(confirmed),\n124\t                _ => {}\n125\t            }\n126\t        }\n127\t        out\n128\t    }\n129\t\n130\t    /// Whether `event` occurs anywhere in the log.\n131\t    pub fn contains(&self, event: &Event) -> bool {\n132\t        self.events().iter().any(|e| e == event)\n133\t    }\n134\t\n135\t    /// Whether any event matches `pred` — for variants carrying a payload a\n136\t    /// test does not want to spell out in full.\n137\t    pub fn any(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> bool {\n138\t        self.events().iter().any(pred)\n139\t    }\n140\t\n141\t    /// Index of the first event matching `pred`, for order assertions.\n142\t    pub fn position(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> Option<usize> {\n143\t        self.events().iter().position(pred)\n144\t    }\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/// Assert the core durability guarantee over an [`EventLog`]: **no bookmark is\n148\t/// persisted before the sink confirmed the data that bookmark claims.**\n149\t///\n150\t/// `records_claimed` maps a persisted bookmark to the number of records it\n151\t/// asserts are durable. A bookmark that claims more than had been confirmed at\n152\t/// the moment it was written is a data-loss bug: a resumed run starts after it\n153\t/// and the unconfirmed records are never read again.\n154\t///\n155\t/// This lives here, rather than inline in each test, so the *same* assertion\n156\t/// can be pointed at a deliberately-wrong event log — which is how the suites\n157\t/// prove the check can actually fail (see this module's tests).\n158\tpub fn assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(\n159\t    log: &EventLog,\n160\t    records_claimed: impl Fn(&Value) -> usize,\n161\t) {\n162\t    let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n163\t    for (i, event) in log.events().into_iter().enumerate() {\n164\t        match event {\n165\t            Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n166\t            Event::StatePut { ref value, .. } => {\n167\t                let claimed = records_claimed(value);\n168\t                assert!(\n169\t                    claimed <= confirmed,\n170\t                    \"durability violated at event {i}: bookmark {value} claims {claimed} \\\n171\t                     records are durable, but only {confirmed} had been confirmed by the sink. \\\n172\t                     A resumed run would start after this bookmark and never re-read the \\\n173\t                     difference.\\nfull log: {:?}\",\n174\t                    log.events()\n175\t                );\n176\t            }\n177\t            _ => {}\n178\t        }\n179\t    }\n180\t}\n181\t\n182\t/// The boundary a script fails at.\n183\t///\n184\t/// Every variant names a real transition the engine makes, and each one is a\n185\t/// place a naive implementation gets the ordering wrong.\n\n... [731 lines truncated] ...\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"55398c64-7c