{"parentUuid":"54ff333e-725c-419e-8090-2d895799bfcf","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01KhKjX624ab34DShvR3xkuN","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"faucet-stream\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Bash\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/c-sikawats/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.2.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":311},"type":"attachment","uuid":"143a1ab4-c9f1-4392-9682-7b2e4d2d8845","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:32:51.559Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/cover-pure-decisions","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"143a1ab4-c9f1-4392-9682-7b2e4d2d8845","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/src/scripted.rs","snippet":"1\t//! Boundary-precise failure injection for the reliability suites (#651\n2\t//! Category A).\n3\t//!\n4\t//! The engine's guarantees are all statements about **ordering across a\n5\t//! boundary** — \"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms\", \"the\n6\t//! staging table is swapped only after a fully successful run\", \"a cancel still\n7\t//! flushes\". A unit test over pure logic cannot observe an ordering, and a\n8\t//! hand-rolled `FailingSink` that fails on *every* call cannot fail at one\n9\t//! specific boundary. So each such test used to re-invent a slightly different\n10\t//! double, and the interesting case — fail on the *third* write, succeed\n11\t//! before and after — was rarely covered.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! This module supplies the two things those tests actually need:\n14\t//!\n15\t//! - [`EventLog`] — one shared, ordered record of everything the sink and the\n16\t//!   state store did. Assertions are then written against the *sequence*\n17\t//!   (`writes_before_each_bookmark`), which is what the guarantee says, rather\n18\t//!   than against a final count, which is what a weaker test checks.\n19\t//! - [`ScriptedSink`] / [`ScriptedStateStore`] — doubles that fail (or stall)\n20\t//!   at exactly one named [`Boundary`] and behave normally everywhere else.\n21\t//!\n22\t//! Both are cheap clones over shared state, so a test keeps a handle to inspect\n23\t//! after moving the double into the pipeline.\n24\t\n25\tuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n26\tuse std::time::Duration;\n27\t\n28\tuse faucet_core::{FaucetError, Sink, StateStore, Value, async_trait, json};\n29\t\n30\t/// One thing that happened, in the order it happened.\n31\t///\n32\t/// The variants deliberately carry the *payload size or identity* rather than\n33\t/// the payload: an assertion about ordering needs to know that write #3 landed\n34\t/// before the bookmark that followed it, not what was in it.\n35\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n36\tpub enum Event {\n37\t    /// `write_batch` (or one of its idempotent / partial variants) succeeded\n38\t    /// with this many records.\n39\t    Write(usize),\n40\t    /// A write was attempted and the script failed it. Carries the record count\n41\t    /// so a test can tell *which* batch was rejected.\n42\t    WriteFailed(usize),\n43\t    /// `flush` succeeded.\n44\t    Flush,\n45\t    /// `flush` was attempted and the script failed it.\n46\t    FlushFailed,\n47\t    /// `write_batch_idempotent` succeeded, carrying the commit token the sink\n48\t    /// stored. The token is the one thing a watermark test must see verbatim.\n49\t    IdempotentWrite { records: usize, token: String },\n50\t    /// `begin_overwrite` succeeded.\n51\t    BeginOverwrite,\n52\t    /// `commit_overwrite` succeeded — the atomic swap actually happened.\n53\t    CommitOverwrite,\n54\t    /// `abort_overwrite` succeeded — the staged data was discarded.\n55\t    AbortOverwrite,\n56\t    /// `StateStore::put` succeeded for `key`, carrying the persisted value so a\n57\t    /// test can assert *which* bookmark was durable at that point.\n58\t    StatePut { key: String, value: Value },\n59\t    /// A `StateStore::put` was attempted and the script failed it.\n60\t    StatePutFailed { key: String },\n61\t    /// `cleanup_scope` ran with this many tracked keys.\n62\t    Cleanup(usize),\n63\t}\n64\t\n65\t/// The shared, ordered event log. Clone it to keep a handle after the doubles\n66\t/// are moved into the pipeline.\n67\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]\n68\tpub struct EventLog(Arc<Mutex<Vec<Event>>>);\n69\t\n70\timpl EventLog {\n71\t    /// A fresh, empty log.\n72\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n73\t        Self::default()\n74\t    }\n75\t\n76\t    /// Append one event. Never blocks on a poisoned lock — a panicking test\n77\t    /// should fail on its own assertion, not on a secondary lock panic.\n78\t    pub fn push(&self, event: Event) {\n79\t        if let Ok(mut g) = self.0.lock() {\n80\t            g.push(event);\n81\t        }\n82\t    }\n83\t\n84\t    /// Every event, in order.\n85\t    pub fn events(&self) -> Vec<Event> {\n86\t        self.0.lock().map(|g| g.clone()).unwrap_or_default()\n87\t    }\n88\t\n89\t    /// Total records across successful writes of every kind.\n90\t    pub fn records_written(&self) -> usize {\n91\t        self.events()\n92\t            .iter()\n93\t            .map(|e| match e {\n94\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => *n,\n95\t                _ => 0,\n96\t            })\n97\t            .sum()\n98\t    }\n99\t\n100\t    /// The values persisted by `StateStore::put`, in order.\n101\t    pub fn bookmarks(&self) -> Vec<Value> {\n102\t        self.events()\n103\t            .iter()\n104\t            .filter_map(|e| match e {\n105\t                Event::StatePut { value, .. } => Some(value.clone()),\n106\t                _ => None,\n107\t            })\n108\t            .collect()\n109\t    }\n110\t\n111\t    /// For each successful `StateStore::put`, how many records had been\n112\t    /// **confirmed written** before it.\n113\t    ///\n114\t    /// This is the shape the core durability guarantee is stated in: a bookmark\n115\t    /// must never be persisted covering data the sink has not confirmed. A test\n116\t    /// asserts the returned counts against the data each bookmark claims.\n117\t    pub fn writes_before_each_bookmark(&self) -> Vec<usize> {\n118\t        let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n119\t        let mut out = Vec::new();\n120\t        for e in self.events() {\n121\t            match e {\n122\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n123\t                Event::StatePut { .. } => out.push(confirmed),\n124\t                _ => {}\n125\t            }\n126\t        }\n127\t        out\n128\t    }\n129\t\n130\t    /// Whether `event` occurs anywhere in the log.\n131\t    pub fn contains(&self, event: &Event) -> bool {\n132\t        self.events().iter().any(|e| e == event)\n133\t    }\n134\t\n135\t    /// Whether any event matches `pred` — for variants carrying a payload a\n136\t    /// test does not want to spell out in full.\n137\t    pub fn any(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> bool {\n138\t        self.events().iter().any(pred)\n139\t    }\n140\t\n141\t    /// Index of the first event matching `pred`, for order assertions.\n142\t    pub fn position(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> Option<usize> {\n143\t        self.events().iter().position(pred)\n144\t    }\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/// Assert the core durability guarantee over an [`EventLog`]: **no bookmark is\n148\t/// persisted before the sink confirmed the data that bookmark claims.**\n149\t///\n150\t/// `records_claimed` maps a persisted bookmark to the number of records it\n151\t/// asserts are durable. A bookmark that claims more than had been confirmed at\n152\t/// the moment it was written is a data-loss bug: a resumed run starts after it\n153\t/// and the unconfirmed records are never read again.\n154\t///\n155\t/// This lives here, rather than inline in each test, so the *same* assertion\n156\t/// can be pointed at a deliberately-wrong event log — which is how the suites\n157\t/// prove the check can actually fail (see this module's tests).\n158\tpub fn assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(\n159\t    log: &EventLog,\n160\t    records_claimed: impl Fn(&Value) -> usize,\n161\t) {\n162\t    let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n163\t    for (i, event) in log.events().into_iter().enumerate() {\n164\t        match event {\n165\t            Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n166\t            Event::StatePut { ref value, .. } => {\n167\t                let claimed = records_claimed(value);\n168\t                assert!(\n169\t                    claimed <= confirmed,\n170\t                    \"durability violated at event {i}: bookmark {value} claims {claimed} \\\n171\t                     records are durable, but only {confirmed} had been confirmed by the sink. \\\n172\t                     A resumed run would start after this bookmark and never re-read the \\\n173\t                     difference.\\nfull log: {:?}\",\n174\t                    log.events()\n175\t                );\n176\t            }\n177\t            _ => {}\n178\t        }\n179\t    }\n180\t}\n181\t\n182\t/// The boundary a script fails at.\n183\t///\n184\t/// Every variant names a real transition the engine makes, and each one is a\n185\t/// place a naive implementation gets the ordering wrong.\n\n... [745 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"48f20327-54f4-4d58-8d69-9bbd16ca735d","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:32:51.866Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/src/scripted.rs changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t//! Boundary-precise failure injection for the reliability suites (#651\n2\t//! Category A).\n3\t//!\n4\t//! The engine's guarantees are all statements about **ordering across a\n5\t//! boundary** — \"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms\", \"the\n6\t//! staging table is swapped only after a fully successful run\", \"a cancel still\n7\t//! flushes\". A unit test over pure logic cannot observe an ordering, and a\n8\t//! hand-rolled `FailingSink` that fails on *every* call cannot fail at one\n9\t//! specific boundary. So each such test used to re-invent a slightly different\n10\t//! double, and the interesting case — fail on the *third* write, succeed\n11\t//! before and after — was rarely covered.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! This module supplies the two things those tests actually need:\n14\t//!\n15\t//! - [`EventLog`] — one shared, ordered record of everything the sink and the\n16\t//!   state store did. Assertions are then written against the *sequence*\n17\t//!   (`writes_before_each_bookmark`), which is what the guarantee says, rather\n18\t//!   than against a final count, which is what a weaker test checks.\n19\t//! - [`ScriptedSink`] / [`ScriptedStateStore`] — doubles that fail (or stall)\n20\t//!   at exactly one named [`Boundary`] and behave normally everywhere else.\n21\t//!\n22\t//! Both are cheap clones over shared state, so a test keeps a handle to inspect\n23\t//! after moving the double into the pipeline.\n24\t\n25\tuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n26\tuse std::time::Duration;\n27\t\n28\tuse faucet_core::{FaucetError, Sink, StateStore, Value, async_trait, json};\n29\t\n30\t/// One thing that happened, in the order it happened.\n31\t///\n32\t/// The variants deliberately carry the *payload size or identity* rather than\n33\t/// the payload: an assertion about ordering needs to know that write #3 landed\n34\t/// before the bookmark that followed it, not what was in it.\n35\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n36\tpub enum Event {\n37\t    /// `write_batch` (or one of its idempotent / partial variants) succeeded\n38\t    /// with this many records.\n39\t    Write(usize),\n40\t    /// A write was attempted and the script failed it. Carries the record count\n41\t    /// so a test can tell *which* batch was rejected.\n42\t    WriteFailed(usize),\n43\t    /// `flush` succeeded.\n44\t    Flush,\n45\t    /// `flush` was attempted and the script failed it.\n46\t    FlushFailed,\n47\t    /// `write_batch_idempotent` succeeded, carrying the commit token the sink\n48\t    /// stored. The token is the one thing a watermark test must see verbatim.\n49\t    IdempotentWrite { records: usize, token: String },\n50\t    /// `begin_overwrite` succeeded.\n51\t    BeginOverwrite,\n52\t    /// `commit_overwrite` succeeded — the atomic swap actually happened.\n53\t    CommitOverwrite,\n54\t    /// `abort_overwrite` succeeded — the staged data was discarded.\n55\t    AbortOverwrite,\n56\t    /// `StateStore::put` succeeded for `key`, carrying the persisted value so a\n57\t    /// test can assert *which* bookmark was durable at that point.\n58\t    StatePut { key: String, value: Value },\n59\t    /// A `StateStore::put` was attempted and the script failed it.\n60\t    StatePutFailed { key: String },\n61\t    /// `cleanup_scope` ran with this many tracked keys.\n62\t    Cleanup(usize),\n63\t}\n64\t\n65\t/// The shared, ordered event log. Clone it to keep a handle after the doubles\n66\t/// are moved into the pipeline.\n67\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]\n68\tpub struct EventLog(Arc<Mutex<Vec<Event>>>);\n69\t\n70\timpl EventLog {\n71\t    /// A fresh, empty log.\n72\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n73\t        Self::default()\n74\t    }\n75\t\n76\t    /// Append one event. Never blocks on a poisoned lock — a panicking test\n77\t    /// should fail on its own assertion, not on a secondary lock panic.\n78\t    pub fn push(&self, event: Event) {\n79\t        if let Ok(mut g) = self.0.lock() {\n80\t            g.push(event);\n81\t        }\n82\t    }\n83\t\n84\t    /// Every event, in order.\n85\t    pub fn events(&self) -> Vec<Event> {\n86\t        self.0.lock().map(|g| g.clone()).unwrap_or_default()\n87\t    }\n88\t\n89\t    /// Total records across successful writes of every kind.\n90\t    pub fn records_written(&self) -> usize {\n91\t        self.events()\n92\t            .iter()\n93\t            .map(|e| match e {\n94\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => *n,\n95\t                _ => 0,\n96\t            })\n97\t            .sum()\n98\t    }\n99\t\n100\t    /// The values persisted by `StateStore::put`, in order.\n101\t    pub fn bookmarks(&self) -> Vec<Value> {\n102\t        self.events()\n103\t            .iter()\n104\t            .filter_map(|e| match e {\n105\t                Event::StatePut { value, .. } => Some(value.clone()),\n106\t                _ => None,\n107\t            })\n108\t            .collect()\n109\t    }\n110\t\n111\t    /// For each successful `StateStore::put`, how many records had been\n112\t    /// **confirmed written** before it.\n113\t    ///\n114\t    /// This is the shape the core durability guarantee is stated in: a bookmark\n115\t    /// must never be persisted covering data the sink has not confirmed. A test\n116\t    /// asserts the returned counts against the data each bookmark claims.\n117\t    pub fn writes_before_each_bookmark(&self) -> Vec<usize> {\n118\t        let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n119\t        let mut out = Vec::new();\n120\t        for e in self.events() {\n121\t            match e {\n122\t                Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n123\t                Event::StatePut { .. } => out.push(confirmed),\n124\t                _ => {}\n125\t            }\n126\t        }\n127\t        out\n128\t    }\n129\t\n130\t    /// Whether `event` occurs anywhere in the log.\n131\t    pub fn contains(&self, event: &Event) -> bool {\n132\t        self.events().iter().any(|e| e == event)\n133\t    }\n134\t\n135\t    /// Whether any event matches `pred` — for variants carrying a payload a\n136\t    /// test does not want to spell out in full.\n137\t    pub fn any(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> bool {\n138\t        self.events().iter().any(pred)\n139\t    }\n140\t\n141\t    /// Index of the first event matching `pred`, for order assertions.\n142\t    pub fn position(&self, pred: impl Fn(&Event) -> bool) -> Option<usize> {\n143\t        self.events().iter().position(pred)\n144\t    }\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/// Assert the core durability guarantee over an [`EventLog`]: **no bookmark is\n148\t/// persisted before the sink confirmed the data that bookmark claims.**\n149\t///\n150\t/// `records_claimed` maps a persisted bookmark to the number of records it\n151\t/// asserts are durable. A bookmark that claims more than had been confirmed at\n152\t/// the moment it was written is a data-loss bug: a resumed run starts after it\n153\t/// and the unconfirmed records are never read again.\n154\t///\n155\t/// This lives here, rather than inline in each test, so the *same* assertion\n156\t/// can be pointed at a deliberately-wrong event log — which is how the suites\n157\t/// prove the check can actually fail (see this module's tests).\n158\tpub fn assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes(\n159\t    log: &EventLog,\n160\t    records_claimed: impl Fn(&Value) -> usize,\n161\t) {\n162\t    let mut confirmed = 0usize;\n163\t    for (i, event) in log.events().into_iter().enumerate() {\n164\t        match event {\n165\t            Event::Write(n) | Event::IdempotentWrite { records: n, .. } => confirmed += n,\n166\t            Event::StatePut { ref value, .. } => {\n167\t                let claimed = records_claimed(value);\n168\t                assert!(\n169\t                    claimed <= confirmed,\n170\t                    \"durability violated at event {i}: bookmark {value} claims {claimed} \\\n171\t                     records are durable, but only {confirmed} had been confirmed by the sink. \\\n172\t                     A resumed run would start after this bookmark and never re-read the \\\n173\t                     difference.\\nfull log: {:?}\",\n174\t                    log.events()\n175\t                );\n176\t            }\n177\t            _ => {}\n178\t        }\n179\t    }\n180\t}\n181\t\n182\t/// The boundary a script fails at.\n183\t///\n184\t/// Every variant names a real transition the engine makes, and each one is a\n185\t/// place a naive implementation gets the ordering wrong.\n\n... [745 lines truncated] ...\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"55398c64-7c