{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","prNumber":659,"prUrl":"https://github.com/faucet-hq/faucet-stream/pull/659","prRepository":"faucet-hq/faucet-stream","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:48:30.379Z"}
{"parentUuid":"d796e03d-f4d1-4ff1-923a-0dc8a25d8d3b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf9uVQufSWYCJ7bKSztxC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D3jShpWAFPVbvpsML8SDaG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ncat > $S/pr651.md <<'BODY'\nImplements the integration-test program in #651.\n\nThe first thing this needed was an honest audit, because the issue's premise — that there is \"no systematic integration-test program\" — turned out to be **partly stale**. The repo already has 292 integration-test files, and much of Categories B, C and D is covered by them. Writing the matrix as specified would have produced a large pile of duplicates. So this PR builds the foundations the program was actually missing, fills the genuinely uncovered categories, and records the audit so the issue's checklist reflects reality.\n\nFull per-item audit is posted as a comment on #651. Summary: **A1/A2/A3/A4/A8, E1, F, G1 were gaps and are now covered; A5/A7 were already covered by `faucet-core`'s own suites; B, C, D are substantially covered by existing files.**\n\n## What the program needed first\n\nA guarantee like *\"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms the data it covers\"* is not a property of any function — it is a property of the **order** in which the engine calls two collaborators. No pure-logic unit test can observe an order, and no per-connector test can either, because it only sees its own side of the boundary.\n\nEvery such test in the repo previously hand-rolled a `FailingSink` that failed on *every* call, so the interesting case — fail on the third write, succeed either side — went untested.\n\n### `faucet_conformance::scripted`\n\n- **`EventLog`** — one shared, ordered record of everything the sink and state store did. Assertions are written against the *sequence*, which is what the guarantee says, rather than a final row count, which is what a weaker test checks and which stays green through a real regression.\n- **`ScriptedSink` / `ScriptedStateStore`** — fail at exactly one named `Boundary` (`Write(n)`, `Flush`, `StatePut(n)`, `CommitOverwrite`) and behave normally everywhere else. Capabilities are advertised **independently**, because several guarantees are *about* the advertisement — including the combination behind the retry-gate defect fixed in #656: token-capable, plain write not replay-safe.\n- **`PagedSource`** — bookmarks *every* page, so writes and bookmarks actually interleave. The existing `CountingSource` bookmarks once at the end, so there is only ever one pair and no ordering to get wrong. `failing_after(n)` injects a read-side fault.\n- **`rearm`** — lets a two-phase crash test reuse **one** sink instance, so its commit tokens survive the \"restart\" exactly as a real destination's would. A second instance would start with an empty token store and quietly turn the interesting test into a trivial one.\n\n### `faucet_conformance::fidelity`\n\nThe shared typed round-trip corpus. Owns the hazard classes centrally — `i64` past 2^53, `-0.0`, embedded quotes and backslashes, empty strings, tz offsets, pre-epoch instants, nested JSON — so a pair's test cannot quietly pick easier data. Rows are matched by key, so a reordering destination is not penalised, and an *invented* row (a duplicate from a bad retry) is reported as a failure rather than tolerated. `Tolerance` makes every allowance an explicit statement about a destination limitation instead of a loose comparison hiding a bug.\n\n## The suites — 51 new tests\n\n| Suite | Guarantee |\n|---|---|\n| `reliability_bookmark_ordering` (8) | **A1** Every bookmark is backed by confirmed writes. A failed write, flush, or state-put each leave the durable bookmark *behind* the failure; resume re-reads exactly the unconfirmed tail with no gap and ≤1 page of duplicates. |\n| `reliability_exactly_once` (6) | **A2** Both mechanisms. Watermark: monotonic distinct tokens, a crash in the commit→state-put window replays **nothing**, a re-run of a completed pipeline writes nothing, a failed write leaves no token to skip on. Keyed: converges with no watermark. |\n| `reliability_lifecycle` (9) | **A3** `begin` precedes the first write, `commit` follows the last; a failed write, failed flush, or **cancel** each abort without ever swapping; a failing commit surfaces instead of reporting success; a suppressed lifecycle leaves every phase to the caller (#552). **A8** A cancelled run flushes what it accepted. |\n| `reliability_cleanup_safety` (8) | **A4** The only feature that deletes rows, so the tests are about when it must *not* fire. |\n| `reliability_fault_injection` (8) | **E1** Read-side faults, plus **termination** under a hard ceiling. |\n| `compat_state_format` (11) | **G1** State written by a release still loads. |\n| `sink/sqlite/tests/fidelity` (4) | **F** The first pair, Docker-free so it gates every PR. |\n\n### Two things worth calling out\n\n**The exactly-once suite has a control arm.** It runs the *identical* injected failure under the default at-least-once mode and asserts that it **does** duplicate. Without that, the exactly-once assertions would not be distinguishing the two modes and could pass for the wrong reason.\n\n**Cleanup's cancelled case needs its own test** because a cancel returns `Ok` by design — the run stopped cleanly and flushed. The obvious check \"did the run succeed?\" would let that delete through, and it is the case where the written-key set is partial and every unwritten row looks stale.\n\n### Termination, not just correctness\n\nEvery run in the fault suite executes under a hard 20s ceiling. A retry loop that never gives up is indistinguishable from a hang from outside, and an operator cannot tell a slow pipeline from a wedged one. The cancelled-slow-sink test asserts the run stops within 5s rather than draining a ~16s backlog.\n\n## Failing-first, per the acceptance criteria\n\nA check that cannot fail is worthless, so each is shown capable of failing:\n\n- The durability guarantee is asserted through `assert_bookmarks_backed_by_writes`, which has three `#[should_panic]` tests feeding it deliberately-wrong logs (bookmark before write; bookmark overclaiming after a partial write).\n- The fidelity comparison has a corrupted-read-back test.\n- The fault suite has a healthy control run — without it, a bug that made every run fail early would leave the suite green.\n- The corpus has a not-silently-empty guard.\n\n## What the new tests found\n\n- **SQLite's auto-mapped path is not type-preserving**, and now says so: no boolean type, so `true`/`false` land as `1`/`0`; negative zero loses its sign through a TEXT-affinity column. Named via `Tolerance::skipping` with the reason, and the observed values are *also* pinned so a change that starts dropping those columns cannot hide behind the tolerance. The JSON-document path, by contrast, is exactly lossless with no tolerance at all.\n- **A whitespace-padded commit token parses, deliberately** — a `CHAR(20)` watermark column pads on read, and rejecting the padded form would make every such stored watermark unreadable, so the engine would see \"nothing committed\" and replay the whole stream. My first test asserted the opposite; the code was right and the test was wrong. The leniency is now pinned with its reason so it is not mistaken for sloppiness and tidied away later.\n\n## CI\n\nNew **required** `Reliability guarantees` job runs the Docker-free suites plus `faucet-core`'s. Deliberately separate from `test`, which covers the whole workspace including every testcontainer suite, takes ~25 min, and is **not** a required check — these guarantees are the ones whose regression corrupts data without failing a run, so they get a gate that cannot be skipped or flake on an image pull. Added time: seconds of test execution.\n\n> Note: this job still needs adding to the branch-protection required set once it has reported on a commit. Per CLAUDE.md, a context must be confirmed to actually report before being marked required.\n\n## Docs\n\n`docs/book/src/operations/reliability-testing.md` — the tier map, how to run each locally (including the colima socket and the x86-only MSSQL caveat), what each suite asserts, and how to add a guarantee, a fidelity pair, or a state fixture. Cross-linked from `docs/contributing/testing.md`, which now explains why these suites are a third kind of test.\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --all`; `clippy -p faucet-conformance -p faucet-sink-sqlite --all-targets --all-features -D warnings`; 134 conformance tests + the sqlite sink suite green; `cargo semver-checks check-release -p faucet-conformance --baseline-rev origin/main` → no semver update required; `mdbook build` clean.\n\nAddresses #651 (Categories A, E1, F, G1 land here; the remaining per-item status is in the audit comment).\nBODY\ngh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --base main --head test/reliability-program \\\n  --title \"test: engine-level reliability program — guarantee suites, fidelity corpus, state-format gates\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr651.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Open the reliability-program PR"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":547303,"output_tokens":3267,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3267,"cache_read_input_tokens":547303,"cache_creation_input_tokens":404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":404},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01D3jShpWAFPVbvpsML8SDaG":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ncat > $S/pr651.md <<'BODY'\nImplements the integration-test program in #651.\n\nThe first thing this needed was an honest audit, because the issue's premise — that there is \"no systematic integration-test program\" — turned out to be **partly stale**. The repo already has 292 integration-test files, and much of Categories B, C and D is covered by them. Writing the matrix as specified would have produced a large pile of duplicates. So this PR builds the foundations the program was actually missing, fills the genuinely uncovered categories, and records the audit so the issue's checklist reflects reality.\n\nFull per-item audit is posted as a comment on #651. Summary: **A1/A2/A3/A4/A8, E1, F, G1 were gaps and are now covered; A5/A7 were already covered by `faucet-core`'s own suites; B, C, D are substantially covered by existing files.**\n\n## What the program needed first\n\nA guarantee like *\"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms the data it covers\"* is not a property of any function — it is a property of the **order** in which the engine calls two collaborators. No pure-logic unit test can observe an order, and no per-connector test can either, because it only sees its own side of the boundary.\n\nEvery such test in the repo previously hand-rolled a `FailingSink` that failed on *every* call, so the interesting case — fail on the third write, succeed either side — went untested.\n\n### `faucet_conformance::scripted`\n\n- **`EventLog`** — one shared, ordered record of everything the sink and state store did. Assertions are written against the *sequence*, which is what the