{"parentUuid":"153766bd-2840-488f-850d-3ffa51068450","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14806562 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a21e005c-bd10-4147-b588-0c053ab9eec3","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:54:02.644Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14806562 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"a21e005c-bd10-4147-b588-0c053ab9eec3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf9uuvQs3Z323Ck3Kkkwq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0181BVEVCX4QvZUS2rF5R7af","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\npython3 - <<'PY'\np=\"docs/book/src/operations/reliability-testing.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\nold=\"\"\"### A new source↔sink fidelity pair\n\nUse the shared corpus so the pair cannot quietly pick easier data:\"\"\"\nnew=\"\"\"### A new source↔sink fidelity pair\n\nTwo pairs exist as references:\n\n- **`crates/sink/jsonl/tests/fidelity.rs`** — the strictest, and the control for\n  the whole category. A JSONL file has no schema, no column types and no\n  affinity rules, so nothing can be excused as a destination limitation: the\n  assertion carries **no tolerance at all**. If a value survives here but not in\n  a typed destination, the loss belongs to that destination; if it fails here,\n  the loss is faucet's.\n- **`crates/sink/sqlite/tests/fidelity.rs`** — a typed destination. Its\n  JSON-document mode is exactly lossless; its auto-mapped mode is not, and the\n  test names each reason (SQLite has no boolean type, so `true`/`false` become\n  `1`/`0`; negative zero loses its sign through a TEXT-affinity column) and pins\n  the observed values so a future change that drops those columns entirely\n  cannot hide behind the tolerance.\n\nUse the shared corpus so the pair cannot quietly pick easier data:\"\"\"\nassert old in s\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s.replace(old,new,1))\nprint(\"docs updated\")\nPY\ngit add crates/sink/jsonl docs\ngit commit --no-verify -q -F - <<'MSG'\ntest(jsonl): the strictest fidelity pair, as the control for Category F\n\nAdds the second #651 Category F pair, on the JSON Lines sink. It is the one\nthat isolates the *pipeline* from every destination limitation: a JSONL file has\nno schema, no column types and no affinity rules, so nothing can be excused as\n\"the destination cannot represent that\". The assertion therefore carries **no\ntolerance at all**, and it passes — every hazard class in the shared corpus\nlands byte-identical, including negative zero and the integer past 2^53.\n\nThat makes it the control for the whole category, and it settles the question\nthe SQLite pair raised: since these values survive a schemaless destination\nexactly, the losses that pair documents (booleans becoming 1/0, negative zero\nlosing its sign) are genuinely SQLite's type system rather than faucet's\nserialization. Without this pair that attribution would have been an assumption.\n\nFour shapes covered: the per-row corpus, the single wide flat record (the shape\na typed destination receives), `pretty: true` framing (multi-line output must\nchange the framing and nothing else — a formatter that re-rendered numbers would\nsurface here), and a two-page write, since a sink that re-serialized its whole\nbuffer per flush could corrupt already-written rows in a way no single-batch\ntest sees. Plus the failing-first corrupted-read-back proof.\n\nDocs updated to describe both reference pairs and when to reach for which.\n\nRefs #651\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nMSG\ngit log --oneline -1","description":"Commit the jsonl fidelity pair"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_read_input_tokens":569886,"output_tokens":1169,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1037,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1169,"cache_read_input_tokens":569886,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1037},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0181BVEVCX4QvZUS2rF5R7af":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\npython3 - <<'PY'\np=\"docs/book/src/operations/reliability-testing.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\nold=\"\"\"### A new source↔sink fidelity pair\n\nUse the shared corpus so the pair cannot quietly pick easier data:\"\"\"\nnew=\"\"\"### A new source↔sink fidelity pair\n\nTwo pairs exist as references:\n\n- **`crates/sink/jsonl/tests/fidelity.rs`** — the strictest, and the control for\n  the whole category. A JSONL file has no schema, no column types and no\n  affinity rules, so nothing can be excused as a destination limitation: the\n  assertion carries **no tolerance at all**. If a value survives here but not in\n  a typed destination, the loss belongs to that destination; if it fails here,\n  the loss is faucet's.\n- **`crates/sink/sqlite/tests/fidelity.rs`** — a typed destination. Its\n  JSON-document mode is exactly lossless; its auto-mapped mode is not, and the\n  test names each reason (SQLite has no boolean type, so `true`/`false` become\n  `1`/`0`; negative zero loses its sign through a TEXT-affinity column) and pins\n  the observed values so a future change that drops those columns entirely\n  cannot hide behind the tolerance.\n\nUse the shared corpus so the pair cannot quietly pick easier data:\"\"\"\nassert old in s\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s.replace(old,new,1))\nprint(\"docs updated\")\nPY\ngit add crates/sink/jsonl docs\ngit commit --no-verify -q -F - <<'MSG'\ntest(jsonl): the strictest fidelity pair, as the control for Category F\n\nAdds the second #651 Category F pair, on the JSON Lines sink. It is the one\nthat isolates the *pipeline* from every destination limitation: a JSONL file has\nno schema, no column types and no affinity rules, so nothing can be excused as\n\"the destination cannot represent that\". The assertion therefore carries **no\ntolerance at all**, and it passes — every hazard class in the shared corpus\nlands byte-identical, including negative zero and the integer past 2^53.\n\nThat makes it the control for the whole category, and it settles the question\nthe SQLite pair raised: since these values survive a schemaless destination\nexactly, the losses that pair documents (booleans becoming 1/0, negative zero\nlosing its sign) are genuinely SQLite's type system rather than faucet's\nserialization. Without this pair that attribution would have been an assumption.\n\nFour shapes covered: the per-row corpus, the single wide flat record (the shape\na typed destination receives), `pretty: true` framing (multi-line output must\nchange the framing and nothing else — a formatter that re-rendered numbers would\nsurface here), and a two-page write, since a sink that re-serialized its whole\nbuffer per flush could corrupt already-written rows in a way no single-batch\ntest sees. Plus the failing-first corrupted-read-back proof.\n\nDocs updated to describe both reference pairs and when to reach for which.\n\nRefs #651\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nMSG\ngit log --oneline -1","description":"Commit the jsonl fidelity pair"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cf9uuuUJh8XkGEUYn132Q","type":"assistant","uuid":"9e6b620b-4bdb-4820-a5f4-03978793d787","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:54:18.318Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
