{"parentUuid":"6ae4c052-f9e8-49d3-8386-8d035b08e35a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAKghasht1jHRo6rgFMf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is systemic — **9 SQL sources** map runtime query failures to `FaucetError::Config`. Worth filing properly:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_read_input_tokens":674873,"output_tokens":2453,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":127},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1508,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2453,"cache_read_input_tokens":674873,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1508},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfAKggxucNryHgpW6RdKP","type":"assistant","uuid":"86e197a7-5091-4cb0-8824-df0b20cd9b61","timestamp":"2026-09-18T05:05:52.318Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/reliability-job-always-runs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"86e197a7-5091-4cb0-8824-df0b20cd9b61","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAKghasht1jHRo6rgFMf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SDft9XPwzd3V77oNpgTCXv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ngh auth switch --user PawanSikawat >/dev/null 2>&1 && gh issue create \\\n  --title \"bug: SQL sources type a runtime query/connection failure as \\`FaucetError::Config\\`\" \\\n  --label bug --label tier-2 \\\n  --body \"$(cat <<'BODY'\n## Summary\n\nNine SQL sources map **runtime** failures — a dropped connection, a server shutdown mid-cursor, a lock timeout — to `FaucetError::Config`, the variant that means *\"your YAML is wrong\"*.\n\nFound by the new container-tier fault-injection suite (#651 Category E1), which stops a Postgres container while a cursor is open:\n\n```\nConfig(\"PostgreSQL query failed: error returned from database: \\\n        terminating connection due to administrator command\")\n```\n\nNothing about that is a configuration problem. The config was correct; the database went away.\n\n## Affected\n\nAll nine map `sqlx`/driver errors straight into `Config`:\n\n`crates/source/{postgres,mysql,mssql,sqlite,duckdb,redshift,bigquery,spanner,mssql-cdc}/src/stream.rs`\n\nIn `postgres` alone:\n\n```rust\n// line 36 — connect\n.map_err(|e| FaucetError::Config(format!(\"PostgreSQL connection failed: {e}\")))?;\n// lines 299, 343 — query, i.e. mid-run\n.map_err(|e| FaucetError::Config(format!(\"PostgreSQL query failed: {e}\")))?;\n```\n\nThe connect-time one at line 36 is arguably defensible: the source connects eagerly in `new()`, so an unreachable server does abort before any data moves, and `faucet validate` / `doctor` surfacing it as config-shaped is not unreasonable. **The query-time ones are not defensible** — by then the run is in flight and the failure is unambiguously operational.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n**Today it is a diagnostics bug, not a reliability one** — I checked before filing. `FaucetError::is_retriable()` returns `false` for both `Config` and `Source`, so the misclassification does not currently change retry behaviour:\n\n```rust\nFaucetError::HttpStatus { status, .. } => *status >= 500 || *status == 429,\nFaucetError::RateLimited(_) => true,\n_ => false,\n```\n\nSo the immediate cost is operator time: an on-call engineer sees `Config` during a database incident and goes to inspect their pipeline YAML instead of their database.\n\n**But it becomes a reliability bug the moment anyone improves retriability**, which is the obvious next step for a dropped connection. A transient connection loss *should* be retriable, and when someone makes `Source`/`Custom` retriable, every SQL source will be silently excluded — because its transient failures are wearing the one variant that must never be retried. The classification is load-bearing precisely because it is the thing a future retry gate will key off (PRINCIPLES §6: the variant is the API).\n\nIt is also the same shape as the audit findings closed in #656: a failure class recoverable from a *type* was instead flattened into a variant chosen for its message.\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **Split connect from query.** Keep an eager-connect failure wherever it genuinely blocks config validation, but route every in-flight failure to a runtime variant.\n2. **Preserve the driver's typed error** rather than stringifying it. `crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/replication.rs` already has the pattern from #656 — `PostgresError { sqlstate, context, message }` carried in `FaucetError::Custom`, with `pg_err(context, e)` and `sqlstate_of(&err)`. Reuse or generalise that so SQLSTATE survives; class `08` (connection exception), `57P01` (admin shutdown) and `40001` (serialization failure) are exactly what a retry gate wants.\n   - `cli/src/serve/history/sql.rs::classify_backend_error` (also #656) is a second worked example, including the SQLite extended-result-code masking.\n3. **Then make the transient classes retriable**, as a separate, deliberate change with its own tests — not folded in here.\n\n### Affected crates / files\n\n- The nine `crates/source/*/src/stream.rs` files above.\n- Possibly a shared helper: this is the third place needing SQLSTATE-preserving error wrapping, so it likely belongs in `faucet-core` (or a `faucet-common-sql`) rather than copied a ninth time.\n- `crates/source/postgres/tests/fault_injection.rs` — currently asserts the *present* behaviour with a comment pointing here; tighten it when this lands.\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n\n- **`faucet validate` / `doctor` must not regress.** They rely on an unreachable server being reported clearly; moving connect-time errors off `Config` must keep those messages actionable.\n- **`FaucetError::Custom` is `Box<dyn Error>`**, so anything downgrading it to a string loses the code again — the CDC crate's comment on this is worth re-reading before starting.\n- **Do not sniff message text to decide the class.** That is the defect #656 removed; take the code from the driver's typed error or leave it absent.\n- **A genuinely bad query** (syntax error, missing relation) is permanent and must stay non-retriable — SQLSTATE `42xxx`. Getting this wrong would retry a typo forever.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An in-flight query/connection failure no longer surfaces as `FaucetError::Config` in any of the nine sources.\n- [ ] The driver's SQLSTATE survives into the error and is readable without parsing a message.\n- [ ] A permanent failure (syntax error, undefined table) is still classified permanent.\n- [ ] `faucet validate` / `doctor` messages for an unreachable server stay actionable — asserted, not assumed.\n- [ ] The container-tier assertion in `crates/source/postgres/tests/fault_injection.rs` is tightened to require the runtime variant.\n- [ ] ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Actually making the transient classes retriable (follow-up, needs its own failure-injection tests).\n- Sinks — worth auditing for the same pattern, but separately.\n\n## References\n\n- Surfaced by the #651 Category E1 container tier.\n- #656 for both the pattern to copy (`pg_err` / `sqlstate_of`) and the principle.\n- Roadmap epic #38.\nBODY\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"File the error-classification bug"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_read_input_tokens":674873,"output_tokens":2453,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":127},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1508,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2453,"cache_read_input_tokens":674873,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1508},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SDft9XPwzd3V77oNpgTCXv":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ngh auth switch --user PawanSikawat >/dev/null 2>&1 && gh issue create \\\n  --title \"bug: SQL sources type a runtime query/connection failure as \\`FaucetError::Config\\`\" \\\n  --label bug --label tier-2 \\\n  --body \"$(cat <<'BODY'\n## Summary\n\nNine SQL sources map **runtime** failures — a dropped connection, a server shutdown mid-cursor, a lock timeout — to `FaucetError::Config`, the variant that means *\"your YAML is wrong\"*.\n\nFound by the new container-tier fault-injection suite (#651 Category E1), which stops a Postgres container while a cursor is open:\n\n```\nConfig(\"PostgreSQL query failed: error returned from database: \\\n        terminating connection due to administrator command\")\n```\n\nNothing about that is a configuration problem. The config was correct; the database went away.\n\n## Affected\n\nAll nine map `sqlx`/driver errors straight into `Config`:\n\n`crates/source/{postgres,mysql,mssql,sqlite,duckdb,redshift,bigquery,spanner,mssql-cdc}/src/stream.rs`\n\nIn `postgres` alone:\n\n```rust\n// line 36 — connect\n.map_err(|e| FaucetError::Config(format!(\"PostgreSQL connection failed: {e}\")))?;\n// lines 299, 343 — query, i.e. mid-run\n.map_err(|e| FaucetError::Config(format!(\"PostgreSQL query failed: {e}\")))?;\n```\n\nThe connect-time one at line 36 is arguably defensible: the source connects eagerly in `new()`, so an unreachable server does abort before any data moves, and `faucet validate` / `doctor` surfacing it as config-shaped is not unreasonable. **The query-time ones are not defensible** — by then the run is in flight and the failure is unambiguously operational.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n**Today it is a diagnostics bug, not a reliability one** — I checked before filing. `FaucetError::is_retriable()` returns `false` for both `Config` and `Source`, so the misclassification does not currently change retry behaviour:\n\n```rust\nFaucetError::HttpStatus { status, .. } => *status >= 500 || *status == 429,\nFaucetError::RateLimited(_) => true,\n_ => false,\n```\n\nSo the immediate cost is operator time: an on-call engineer sees `Config` during a database incident and goes to inspect their pipeline YAML instead of their database.\n\n**But it becomes a reliability bug the moment anyone improves retriability**, which is the obvious next step for a dropped connection. A transient connection loss *should* be retriable, and when someone makes `Source`/`Custom` retriable, every SQL source will be silently excluded — because its transient failures are wearing the one variant that must never be retried. The classification is load-bearing precisely because it is the thing a future retry gate will key off (PRINCIPLES §6: the variant is the API).\n\nIt is also the same shape as the audit findings closed in #656: a failure class recoverable from a *type* was instead flattened into a variant chosen for its message.\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **Split connect from query.** Keep an eager-connect failure wherever it genuinely blocks config validation, but route every in-flight failure to a runtime variant.\n2. **Preserve the driver's typed error** rather than stringifying it. `crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/replication.rs` already has the pattern from #656 — `PostgresError { sqlstate, context, message }` carried in `FaucetError::Custom`, with `pg_err(context, e)` and `sqlstate_of(&err)`. Reuse or generalise that so SQLSTATE survives; class `08` (connection exception), `57P01` (admin shutdown) and `40001` (serialization failure) are exactly what a retry gate wants.\n   - `cli/src/serve/history/sql.rs::classify_backend_error` (also #656) is a second worked example, including the SQLite extended-result-code masking.\n3. **Then make the transient classes retriable**, as a separate, deliberate change with its own tests — not folded in here.\n\n### Affected crates / files\n\n- The nine `crates/source/*/src/stream.rs` files above.\n- Possibly a shared helper: this is the third place needing SQLSTATE-preserving error wrapping, so it likely belongs in `faucet-core` (or a `faucet-common-sql`) rather than copied a ninth time.\n- `crates/source/postgres/tests/fault_injection.rs` — currently asserts the *present* behaviour with a comment pointing here; tighten it when this lands.\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n\n- **`faucet validate` / `doctor` must not regress.** They rely on an unreachable server being reported clearly; moving connect-time errors off `Config` must keep those messages actionable.\n- **`FaucetError::Custom` is `Box<dyn Error>`**, so anything downgrading it to a string loses the code again — the CDC crate's comment on this is worth re-reading before starting.\n- **Do not sniff message text to decide the class.** That is the defect #656 removed; take the code from the driver's typed error or leave it absent.\n- **A genuinely bad query** (syntax error, missing relation) is permanent and must stay non-retriable — SQLSTATE `42xxx`. Getting this wrong would retry a typo forever.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An in-flight query/connection failure no longer surfaces as `FaucetError::Config` in any of the nine sources.\n- [ ] The driver's SQLSTATE survives into the error and is readable without parsing a message.\n- [ ] A permanent failure (syntax error, undefined table) is still classified permanent.\n- [ ] `faucet validate` / `doctor` messages for an unreachable server stay actionable — asserted, not assumed.\n- [ ] The container-tier assertion in `crates/source/postgres/tests/fault_injection.rs` is tightened to require the runtime variant.\n- [ ] ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Actually making the transient classes retriable (follow-up, needs its own failure-injection tests).\n- Sinks — worth auditing for the same pattern, but separately.\n\n## References\n\n- Surfaced by the #651 Category E1 container tier.\n- #656 for both the pattern to copy (`pg_err` / `sqlstate_of`) and the principle.\n- Roadmap epic #38.\nBODY\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"File the error-classification bug"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfAKggxucNryHgpW6RdKP","type":"assistant","uuid":"1b519a56-b062-4a23-998d-fb76e074a96c","timestamp":"2026-09-18T05:06:22.556Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/reliability-job-always-runs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
