{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","prNumber":661,"prUrl":"https://github.com/faucet-hq/faucet-stream/pull/661","prRepository":"faucet-hq/faucet-stream","timestamp":"2026-09-18T05:10:25.745Z"}
{"parentUuid":"025ba97f-fd1a-42dd-bb64-e0b67585d5ac","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAL3ERaL1a8sPRC3y3N9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013RrRiZ1JZc8tySMQMUxd5X","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ncat > $S/pr663.md <<'BODY'\nFinishes #651. Every remaining item is closed — nothing deferred.\n\nFollows #660 (foundations + Categories A/E1-engine/F-groundwork/G1) and #661 (making the gate always report).\n\n## A6 — the drift-policy matrix (8 tests, Docker-free, **required** tier)\n\n`core::drift` unit-tests the *classification*, and each sink tests its own `evolve_schema`. Neither covered the thing an operator actually configures: what the pipeline **does** when a real source starts sending a column the destination lacks.\n\nAll five `on_drift` arms now run through the real `Pipeline::run` against a destination that genuinely evolves, because each arm has a *different* silent wrong answer:\n\n| Policy | The wrong answer nobody would notice |\n|---|---|\n| `warn` | dropping the page instead of writing it |\n| `evolve` | writing without the DDL, so the new column is lost |\n| `ignore` | failing the run, or erroring on the unknown column |\n| `quarantine` | writing the drifting rows anyway |\n| `fail` | writing the page and *then* raising |\n\n`evolve` asserts the value actually lands in the new column — DDL without data is the silent half-failure — and that a second run does not re-apply the DDL.\n\nTwo came out of the work rather than the plan: a control proving a *non-drifting* page is untouched by every policy (without it the strict arms would break every ordinary pipeline and the suite wouldn't notice), and `quarantine` without a DLQ being refused **before any data moves**. I found the latter because the control arm failed without a DLQ — the gate is at config time, not per page, which is the right place for it.\n\n## F — four more fidelity pairs (18 tests)\n\nOne shared corpus, five destinations. What they establish *together* is more than any one does alone:\n\n| Destination | Result |\n|---|---|\n| **jsonl** | Exactly lossless, **zero tolerance**. The control: no schema, so no excuse. |\n| **sqlite** | JSON mode lossless; auto-map loses booleans (`1`/`0`) and `-0.0`'s sign |\n| **postgres** | Typed columns **fully faithful** — `DOUBLE PRECISION` keeps the sign bit; JSONB does not |\n| **mysql** | Typed columns faithful; backslashes survive despite MySQL treating them as escapes |\n| **mongodb** | BSON **fully faithful**, `-0.0` included; large integers stored as Int64, not Double |\n\nThe Postgres result is the instructive one. JSONB normalises numbers through `numeric`, which has no signed zero — so **\"Postgres loses negative zero\" is false in general and true for the document path**. A dedicated test writes the same `-0.0` to both a `DOUBLE PRECISION` column and a JSONB column in one row and asserts both outcomes, so the distinction is pinned rather than living in a comment.\n\nMongoDB's large-integer test reads back as *canonical* extended JSON specifically so the chosen BSON **type** is checked (`$numberLong`), not merely the value — a `Double` that happens to round-trip a small integer would pass a value-only check and fail on the first number past 2^53.\n\nMySQL's backslash test confirms the sink binds parameters rather than building literals, which is the hazard `faucet_core::sql_literal`'s doc warns about for backslash-escaping dialects.\n\n## E1 — the container tier (5 tests)\n\nStops the database container while a cursor is open. That is a **harder cut than a proxy toxic** — the socket dies with no FIN — and it needs no extra image, so the suite runs anywhere Docker does. The three obligations match the engine tier: the run fails rather than reporting success on partial data, it fails with a typed error naming the cause, and it **terminates** inside a hard ceiling.\n\nWorth noting how this test got written: the first version raced. 40k small rows streamed in before the 400ms cut, the run completed, and it \"failed\" against a perfectly healthy run. Rather than tune the delay I made the *server* the bottleneck with `pg_sleep` per row, which removes the timing assumption instead of hiding it.\n\n## Found on the way — filed as #662\n\nNine SQL sources type **runtime** query and connection failures as `FaucetError::Config` — the variant meaning *\"your YAML is wrong\"* — when the config was fine and the database went away:\n\n```\nConfig(\"PostgreSQL query failed: error returned from database: \\\n        terminating connection due to administrator command\")\n```\n\nI checked the blast radius before filing. `is_retriable()` is `false` for both `Config` and `Source`, so **today this changes no retry behaviour** — it is a diagnostics bug that sends an on-call engineer to their YAML during a database incident. But it becomes a reliability bug the moment a dropped connection is made retriable, which is the obvious next step, because `Config` is the one variant that must never be retried.\n\nNot fixed here: changing an error variant across nine crates is a behaviour change deserving its own review, and #656 already established the pattern to reuse (`pg_err` / `sqlstate_of`). The test asserts the guarantee that holds regardless — typed error, names the connection, terminates, no unbacked bookmark — and separately flags the variant, so it **tightens when #662 lands** rather than entrenching the defect.\n\n## Branch protection\n\n`Reliability guarantees` is now in the required set, added after #661 made the job always report. The granular `PATCH` endpoint was used, preserving all eight existing contexts:\n\n```\n[\"API stability (cargo-semver-checks)\", \"CLI build (full)\", …, \"Coverage\", \"Docs\",\n \"Reliability guarantees\"]   strict: false\n```\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --all`; `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` across all six touched crates; every new suite green locally against colima (`drift_policy_matrix` 8, `postgres/fidelity` 4, `mysql/fidelity` 4, `mongodb/fidelity` 5, `postgres/fault_injection` 5); `mdbook build` clean.\n\nCloses #651\nBODY\ngh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --base main --head test/reliability-program-phase2 \\\n  --title \"test: finish the reliability program — drift matrix, fidelity pairs, container faults\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr663.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Open the phase-2 PR"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":369,"cache_read_input_tokens":688629,"output_tokens":2335,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":369,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2335,"cache_read_input_tokens":688629,"cache_creation_input_tokens":369,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":369},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013RrRiZ1JZc8tySMQMUxd5X":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ncat > $S/pr663.md <<'BODY'\nFinishes #651. Every remaining item is closed — nothing deferred.\n\nFollows #660 (foundations + Categories A/E1-engine/F-groundwork/G1) and #661 (making the gate always report).\n\n## A6 — the drift-policy matrix (8 tests, Docker-free, **required** tier)\n\n`core::drift` unit-tests the *classification*, and each sink tests its own `evolve_schema`. Neither covered the thing an operator actually configures: what the pipeline **does** when a real source starts sending a column the destination lacks.\n\nAll five `on_drift` arms now run through the real `Pipeline::run` against a destination that genuinely evolves, because each arm has a *different* silent wrong answer:\n\n| Policy | The wrong answer nobody would notice |\n|---|---|\n| `warn` | dropping the page instead of writing it |\n| `evolve` | writing without the DDL, so the new column is lost |\n| `ignore` | failing the run, or erroring on the unknown column |\n| `quarantine` | writing the drifting rows anyway |\n| `fail` | writing the page and *then* raising |\n\n`evolve` asserts the value actually lands in the new column — DDL without data is the silent half-failure — and that a second run does not re-apply the DDL.\n\nTwo came out of the work rather than the plan: a control proving a *non-drifting* page is untouched by every policy (without it the strict arms would break every ordinary pipeline and the suite wouldn't notice), and `quarantine` without a DLQ being refused **before any data moves**. I found the latter because the control arm failed without a DLQ — the gate is at config time, not per page, which is the right place for it.\n\n## F — four more fidelity pairs (18 tests)\n\nOne shared corpus, five destinations. What they establish *together* is more than any one does alone:\n\n| Destination | Result |\n|---|---|\n| **jsonl** | Exactly lossless, **zero tolerance**. The control: no schema, so no excuse. |\n| **sqlite** | JSON mode lossless; auto-map loses booleans (`1`/`0`) and `-0.0`'s sign |\n| **postgres** | Typed columns **fully faithful** — `DOUBLE PRECISION` keeps the sign bit; JSONB does not |\n| **mysql** | Typed columns faithful; backslashes survive despite MySQL treating them as escapes |\n| **mongodb** | BSON **fully faithful**, `-0.0` included; large integers stored as Int64, not Double |\n\nThe Postgres result is the instructive one. JSONB normalises numbers through `numeric`, which has no signed zero — so **\"Postgres loses negative zero\" is false in general and true for the document path**. A dedicated test writes the same `-0.0` to both a `DOUBLE PRECISION` column and a JSONB column in one row and asserts both outcomes, so the distinction is pinned rather than living in a comment.\n\nMongoDB's large-integer test reads back as *canonical* extended JSON specifically so the chosen BSON **type** is checked (`$numberLong`), not merely the value — a `Double` that happens to round-trip a small integer would pass a value-only check and fail on the first number past 2^53.\n\nMySQL's backslash test confirms the sink binds parameters rather than building literals, which is the hazard `faucet_core::sql_literal`'s doc warns about for backslash-escaping dialects.\n\n## E1 — the container tier (5 tests)\n\nStops the database container while a cursor is open. That is a **harder cut than a proxy toxic** — the socket dies with no FIN — and it needs no extra image, so the suite runs anywhere Docker does. The three obligations match the engine tier: the run fails rather than reporting success on partial data, it fails with a typed error naming the cause, and it **terminates** inside a hard ceiling.\n\nWorth noting how this test got written: the first version raced. 40k small rows streamed in before the 400ms cut, the run completed, and it \"failed\" against a perfectly healthy run. Rather than tune the delay I made the *server* the bottleneck with `pg_sleep` per row, which removes the timing assumption instead of hiding it.\n\n## Found on the way — filed as #662\n\nNine SQL sources type **runtime** query and connection failures as `FaucetError::Config` — the variant meaning *\"your YAML is wrong\"* — when the config was fine and the database went away:\n\n```\nConfig(\"PostgreSQL query failed: error returned from database: \\\n        terminating connection due to administrator command\")\n```\n\nI checked the blast radius before filing. `is_retriable()` is `false` for both `Config` and `Source`, so **today this changes no retry behaviour** — it is a diagnostics bug that sends an on-call engineer to their YAML during a database incident. But it becomes a reliability bug the moment a dropped connection is made retriable, which is the obvious next step, because `Config` is the one variant that must never be retried.\n\nNot fixed here: changing an error variant across nine crates is a behaviour change deserving its own review, and #656 already established the pattern to reuse (`pg_err` / `sqlstate_of`). The test asserts the guarantee that holds regardless — typed error, names the connection, terminates, no unbacked bookmark — and separately flags the variant, so it **tightens when #662 lands** rather than entrenching the defect.\n\n## Branch protection\n\n`Reliability guarantees` is now in the required set, added after #661 made the job always report. The granular `PATCH` endpoint was used, preserving all eight existing contexts:\n\n```\n[\"API stability (cargo-semver-checks)\", \"CLI build (full)\", …, \"Coverage\", \"Docs\",\n \"Reliability guarantees\"]   strict: false\n```\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --all`; `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` across all six touched crates; every new suite green locally against colima (`drift_policy_matrix` 8, `postgres/fidelity` 4, `mysql/fidelity` 4, `mongodb/fidelity` 5, `postgres/fault_injection` 5); `mdbook build` clean.\n\nCloses #651\nBODY\ngh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --base main --head test/reliability-program-phase2 \\\n  --title \"test: finish the reliability program — drift matrix, fidelity pairs, container faults\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr663.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Open the phase-2 PR"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfAL3Dt553RWzhP8thBrf","type":"assistant","uuid":"2bdfaf4e-6f24-4cf6-92be-c2431ecb1526","timestamp":"2026-09-18T05:10:54.750Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program-phase2","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
