{"parentUuid":"39f4552c-5c74-4b0d-a3fd-c8c4a909e324","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAND6Z4jTr9GygPakCto","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":848,"cache_read_input_tokens":700404,"output_tokens":2551,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1198},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":848,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2551,"cache_read_input_tokens":700404,"cache_creation_input_tokens":848,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":848},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfAND5xb9PrVag6WjQXuC","type":"assistant","uuid":"5e50a239-b5d5-4be5-b621-4435f7d60c31","timestamp":"2026-09-18T05:39:12.331Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program-phase2","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"5e50a239-b5d5-4be5-b621-4435f7d60c31","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAND6Z4jTr9GygPakCto","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Short answer: **no — I'd not adopt BSON as the internal record format.** The reasoning isn't about BSON's quality; it's that the premise doesn't hold for this architecture. Three things I checked:\n\n**1. There is no source→sink wire format to replace.**\n\nPages move as `Vec<Value>` *in-process*. `Pipeline::run` hands the source's page straight to the sink in memory — nothing is serialized between them. So BSON wouldn't replace a serialization step, it would **add two**: source driver → BSON, then BSON → sink driver. Today most connectors go driver → `Value` → driver, and `Value` is what every intermediate layer already speaks.\n\n**2. The fidelity argument for BSON is mostly false here — I just measured it.**\n\nThe tests I wrote today show `serde_json::Value` is **exactly lossless** for every hazard class: `i64::MAX`, `i64::MIN`, the integer past 2^53, `-0.0` with its sign bit, unicode, embedded quotes and backslashes, empty strings. Zero tolerance, on the JSONL pair.\n\nThat surprises people because they assume JSON means \"everything is an f64\". It doesn't — `serde_json::Number` holds `u64`, `i64`, and `f64` as *distinct* variants. So the classic precision loss never happens in our value path. MongoDB's own BSON pair passed with zero tolerance too — the two formats are equally faithful for our corpus.\n\n**3. The types JSON genuinely lacks are already handled — with exact precision.**\n\nThis is the part I'd have expected to be the real argument for BSON, and it's already solved by convention:\n\n```rust\n// crates/source/postgres/src/stream.rs:111\n// NUMERIC / DECIMAL → string, preserving exact precision.\nif let Ok(v) = row.try_get::<sqlx::types::BigDecimal, _>(col_name) {\n    return Value::String(v.to_string());\n}\n```\n\nDecimals travel as strings and are bound back as text so the destination re-parses them into `NUMERIC`. Same for UUID, and `BYTEA` → base64. No precision is lost.\n\n**What switching would actually cost:** every connector's encode/decode, plus the whole middle of the engine — the DuckDB SQL transform, the WASM transform, the JSONPath extractor, the DLQ envelope, quality/contract/masking passes, lineage facets, `faucet test` fixtures, and the committed JSON Schema. That's most of 92 crates, for no fidelity gain. And BSON's type set is Mongo-shaped (`ObjectId`, `Timestamp`) — wrong vocabulary for a general-purpose tool.\n\n**Where a binary format *is* the right answer — and already exists.** If the goal is throughput on analytic transfers, that's `faucet_core::columnar`, which moves Arrow `RecordBatch`es source→sink without touching `Value` at all. Arrow is the better pick than BSON for this: it's columnar (so it actually speeds up bulk movement, which BSON's document-at-a-time layout does not), it has `Decimal128`/`Decimal256` and tz-aware timestamps natively, and it's what every warehouse and query engine already speaks. There's also `native` for byte passthrough where neither representation is needed.\n\n**The one real weakness worth fixing.** It isn't the encoding — it's that \"this string is a decimal\" is a *convention*, not a *type*. A sink receiving `\"123.45\"` can't tell an exact decimal from ordinary text, so it relies on the destination column's type to disambiguate. That's fine for SQL→SQL, and thinner for a schemaless destination, where a decimal silently becomes a string forever. A typed decimal (or a schema-level annotation the `contract` layer could carry) would close it without touching the record format.\n\nWant me to file that as an issue? It's a small, well-scoped improvement, and unlike a format migration it has a real payoff. I'd also add decimal and binary rows to the fidelity corpus so any future change there is measured rather than assumed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":848,"cache_read_input_tokens":700404,"output_tokens":2551,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1198},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":848,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2551,"cache_read_input_tokens":700404,"cache_creation_input_tokens":848,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":848},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfAND5xb9PrVag6WjQXuC","type":"assistant","uuid":"8b17a857-b5e3-42e4-881f-6dfe50cf567b","timestamp":"2026-09-18T05:39:30.761Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program-phase2","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
