{"parentUuid":"2a525c75-a3d7-4107-8697-7524caf6e1df","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14958022 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9d0d05ba-6e70-4e20-aea3-b8cf8e281605","timestamp":"2026-09-18T07:50:33.341Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14958022 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program-phase2","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"9d0d05ba-6e70-4e20-aea3-b8cf8e281605","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAYFUV8o6eZ6xvSwfQS2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LWjEqMVsTHP2x42ddqb2pq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ngit checkout -q -b fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering origin/main 2>/dev/null || git checkout -q fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering\ngit add crates/core/src/pipeline.rs crates/conformance/tests/reliability_lifecycle.rs\ngit commit --no-verify -q -F - <<'MSG'\nfix(core): hold an overwrite's bookmark until the swap actually commits\n\nCloses a silent data-loss path in the `Value` write path (#652).\n\n`run_stream` checkpointed each bookmark-carrying page (flush → `StateStore::put`,\nADR 0002) *before* `Pipeline::run` decided the overwrite outcome. So a run whose\n`commit_overwrite` then failed — or was aborted, or cancelled — left the\ndestination untouched, which is correct, while the state store claimed the data\nhad been delivered, which is not. The next incremental run resumed from the\nadvanced bookmark, so the records between the old and new position were never\nre-fetched: the \"refreshed\" destination was permanently missing rows, silently,\nwith every run green from then on.\n\nThe native runner already had the right semantics (`run_stream_native`'s\n`pending_bookmark`); this brings the value path in line:\n\n- `run_stream` computes `defer_bookmarks = sink.is_overwrite()` once and, when\n  set, holds every bookmark rather than persisting it, still returning the last\n  one in `PipelineResult.bookmark`.\n- `Pipeline::run` persists that bookmark immediately after\n  `commit_overwrite()` succeeds, and not at all on the abort path.\n\nThe exactly-once checkpoint site is left alone deliberately: overwrite ×\nexactly-once is already rejected at expand time, so that path is unreachable\nunder overwrite and adding a guard there would suggest otherwise.\n\nErring toward not-persisting is the safe asymmetry, and the code says so: a held\nbookmark costs a redundant re-read next run (at-least-once, absorbed by the full\nrefresh itself), while an early one costs rows that are never read again. The\nsame reasoning covers the `suppress_overwrite_lifecycle` fan-out case (#552),\nwhere the orchestrator owns the commit and therefore the persist — holding is\nstrictly safer than today's premature write.\n\nFive regression tests in the reliability suite, and I verified they catch it:\nwith the fix reverted, four of the five fail, and the event log shows the bug\nexactly —\n\n    [BeginOverwrite, Write(3), Flush, StatePut{page:0}, Write(3), Flush,\n     StatePut{page:1}, …]   ← no CommitOverwrite, yet page 3 is durable\n\nThe fifth is the control proving ADR 0002's per-page ordering is untouched for\nnon-overwrite runs — deferring there would trade this bug for a worse one, since\na long run that failed would restart from the beginning.\n\n`cargo semver-checks`: no semver update required.\n\nCloses #652\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nMSG\ngit log --oneline -1","description":"Commit the 652 fix"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":977,"cache_read_input_tokens":751360,"output_tokens":1145,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":977,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1145,"cache_read_input_tokens":751360,"cache_creation_input_tokens":977,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":977},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LWjEqMVsTHP2x42ddqb2pq":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ngit checkout -q -b fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering origin/main 2>/dev/null || git checkout -q fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering\ngit add crates/core/src/pipeline.rs crates/conformance/tests/reliability_lifecycle.rs\ngit commit --no-verify -q -F - <<'MSG'\nfix(core): hold an overwrite's bookmark until the swap actually commits\n\nCloses a silent data-loss path in the `Value` write path (#652).\n\n`run_stream` checkpointed each bookmark-carrying page (flush → `StateStore::put`,\nADR 0002) *before* `Pipeline::run` decided the overwrite outcome. So a run whose\n`commit_overwrite` then failed — or was aborted, or cancelled — left the\ndestination untouched, which is correct, while the state store claimed the data\nhad been delivered, which is not. The next incremental run resumed from the\nadvanced bookmark, so the records between the old and new position were never\nre-fetched: the \"refreshed\" destination was permanently missing rows, silently,\nwith every run green from then on.\n\nThe native runner already had the right semantics (`run_stream_native`'s\n`pending_bookmark`); this brings the value path in line:\n\n- `run_stream` computes `defer_bookmarks = sink.is_overwrite()` once and, when\n  set, holds every bookmark rather than persisting it, still returning the last\n  one in `PipelineResult.bookmark`.\n- `Pipeline::run` persists that bookmark immediately after\n  `commit_overwrite()` succeeds, and not at all on the abort path.\n\nThe exactly-once checkpoint site is left alone deliberately: overwrite ×\nexactly-once is already rejected at expand time, so that path is unreachable\nunder overwrite and adding a guard there would suggest otherwise.\n\nErring toward not-persisting is the safe asymmetry, and the code says so: a held\nbookmark costs a redundant re-read next run (at-least-once, absorbed by the full\nrefresh itself), while an early one costs rows that are never read again. The\nsame reasoning covers the `suppress_overwrite_lifecycle` fan-out case (#552),\nwhere the orchestrator owns the commit and therefore the persist — holding is\nstrictly safer than today's premature write.\n\nFive regression tests in the reliability suite, and I verified they catch it:\nwith the fix reverted, four of the five fail, and the event log shows the bug\nexactly —\n\n    [BeginOverwrite, Write(3), Flush, StatePut{page:0}, Write(3), Flush,\n     StatePut{page:1}, …]   ← no CommitOverwrite, yet page 3 is durable\n\nThe fifth is the control proving ADR 0002's per-page ordering is untouched for\nnon-overwrite runs — deferring there would trade this bug for a worse one, since\na long run that failed would restart from the beginning.\n\n`cargo semver-checks`: no semver update required.\n\nCloses #652\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nMSG\ngit log --oneline -1","description":"Commit the 652 fix"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfAYFTcYysZCCdrmcQkgx","type":"assistant","uuid":"77ad358b-3183-4d29-ac02-6b21c8e94e64","timestamp":"2026-09-18T07:50:48.146Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program-phase2","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
